\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korean won down as bank worries rock shares\b0\par\par\uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2009\par 02:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Won down as economy, financial sector woes hit stocks\par \par \uc2 * Further falls seen on risk aversion\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caution growing (Updates to domestic close)\par \par \uc2 By Cheon Jong-woo\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Jan 20 (Reuters) - The South Korean won fell against the dollar on Tuesday as fresh worries about the health of global banks and the spreading impact of a worldwide recession on Asia's fourth-largest economy hit Seoul shares.\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> came under further pressure from expected dollar demand from overseas investors seen pulling out money deposited for a new share issue by Hynix Semiconductor after many failed to buy the chipmaker's stocks.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to remain weak as more investors are seeking safer assets on persistent worries about grim corporate earnings amid a global recession, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "The won is more likely to weaken past the support line of 1,380 and probably to 1,400 (versus the dollar) soon, as stock markets in the world may fall further on risk aversion," said Jeong My-young, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Samsung Futures Inc.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at 1,374.4/4.6 per dollar as of 0600 GMT, compared with Monday's domestic close <KRW=KFTC> of 1,362.5.\par \par \uc2 Seoul shares <.KS11> ended down 2.07 percent as foreign investors sold a net 72.1 billion won worth of stocks in the country's main exchange.\par \par \uc2 Adding to evidence of a slowdown in the South Korean economy, data showed the country's exports to China, its biggest export market, and the European Union fell by more than a third each in December from a year ago. [IDnSEO337548]\par \par \uc2 With growing expectations for the won's further fall, market players saw more possibility for dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If the won breaks through the 1,400 line, it will go to 1,500 soon. That will make it difficult for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates to boost the economy," said an analyst at a local futures firm, asking not to be identified as he was not authorised to speak to the press.\par \par \uc2 A weaker won usually lifts import prices, eventually boosting consumer inflation, which is seen denting already ailing domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the Bank of Korea cut interest rates to a new low and suggested more cuts to help fend off a recession.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the won may find some support from demand related to LG Display's supply deal with Apple Inc , dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, LG Display said it had a signed a contract to supply LCD panels to Apple for five years and it would receive a $500 million advance in January.[ID:nPEK176050]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       0600 GMT     0325 GMT   prev close\par  Won <KRW=>           1,374.4/4.6  1,377.7/8.4      1,362.5\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>    15.2187/60   15.2519/13   14.9863/56\par  KOSPI <.KS11>           1,126.81     1,119.54     1,150.65\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Lee Soo-jung; Editing by Jonathan Hopfner)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090123e51k0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppic