\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won extends gains on stocks, reserves data\b0\par\par\uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2009\par 01:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to midday)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Jan 5 (Reuters) - The South Korean won extended gains by up to 2 percent against the dollar on Monday as foreigners bought more local shares and after data showed the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves advanced in December.\par \par \uc2 Market players reduced dollar holdings as fewer importers chased the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for settlements, providing further support to the won <KRW=>.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at 1,302.0/3.2 per dollar as of 0300 GMT, compared with Friday's domestic close <KRW=KFTC> of 1,321.0. It gained to as high as 1,294.8.\par \par \uc2 "Investors booked profits from the recent dollar rise and built up dollar-short positions on higher local shares," said a local bank dealer.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid over 5 percent as the government provided a gloomy export outlook for this year and on expectations for less dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the authorities.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose in December for the first time in nine months as investment gains and rises in non-dollar assets more than offset \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nSEO359371]\par \par \uc2 Seoul shares <.KS11> rose 1.21 percent as foreign investors bought a net 155 billion won worth of stocks in the country's main exchange.\par \par \uc2 They purchased a combined net 303.7 billion won during the previous three consecutive sessions, helping the country's balance of payments improve.\par \par \uc2 Last year, foreigners sold a combined net 33.6 trillion won in shares, their highest annual net sales ever, putting pressure on the won.\par \par \uc2 On the corporate front, \uc2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering\uc2  , the world's third-largest shipbuilder, said it expected to win new orders worth more than $10 billion this year, compared with $11.8 billion in 2008. [ID:nSEL000422]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       0300 GMT     0035 GMT   prev close\par  Won <KRW=>           1,302.0/3.2  1,310.8/1.3      1,321.0\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>    14.1565/72   14.2404/59   14.4455/30\par  KOSPI <.KS11>           1,171.40     1,176.22     1,157.40\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Cheon Jong-woo and Lee Soo-jung, Editing by Ken Wills)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090108e5150001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
