\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Singapore dollar, won weighed down by econ fears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2009\par 05:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jan 2 (Reuters) - The South Korean won and Singapore dollar slid on Friday on concerns about the countries' growth outlooks as the global downturn looked set to further hit Asia's export-dependent economies.\par \par \uc2 In 2008, many Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had their worst year in a decade, hit by waves of sell-offs in regional stock and bonds as the global financial crisis spooked investors to flee to safer assets.\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> lost more than 5 percent on Friday to 1,330.9 per dollar, its weakness since Dec. 24, on the government's grim export forecast for 2009 and as the market expected the authorities to cut dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's December exports slid a worse-than-expected 17.4 percent from a year earlier and the government said 2009 exports would probably grow only one percent, marking the weakest pace in eight years. [ID:nL179089]\par \par \uc2 The data fuelled market expectations of another interest rate cut when the Bank of Korea reviews monetary policy on Jan. 9.\par \par \uc2 Most Asian markets were closed on Thursday for the New Year holiday and those in China, Taiwan, Indonesia, Thailand and the Philippines remained shut on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell as far as 1.4555 per U.S. dollar, down almost 1.5 percent from Wednesday's close, after the government cut its economic outlook for 2009 to between a decline of 2 percent and growth of 1 percent. [ID:nSIN411254].\par \par \uc2 "The figures should help weaken the Singapore dollar," said a Singapore-based trader, who reckoned investors were buying back the U.S. dollar on the belief that the Singapore dollar is near the strong end of its trading band managed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  steers monetary policy by managing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed traded-weighted band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than adjusting interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Kit Wei Zheng, an economist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , expects Singapore's economy to contract an annual 2.8 percent in 2009, the most severe recession in city-state's history.\par \par \uc2 In response, the government is likely to unveil an aggressive fiscal stimulus later this month while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably let the Singapore dollar weaken by re-centring its trading band downward in April, when it reviews policy, Kit said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "Although the current recession is due to a contraction in demand, a modest weakening of the Singapore dollar's nominal effective exchange rate (NEER) could still provide some relief for exporters," Kit said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> fell 0.6 percent to 3.4725 per dollar, taking its cue from the Singapore dollar.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard to make a call on the long-term trend at this stage but we expect dollar/ringgit to move higher in the short run," said a trader in Kuala Lumpur, who expected the ringgit to move between 3.46-3.48 for the day.\par \par \uc2 The ringgit in six-month offshore non-deliverable forwards <MYRNDFOR=> fell as low as 3.4910 per dollar, implying a fall of nearly one percent from Friday's spot rate. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change as of 0427 GMT since Wednesday \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen        91.13          90.60       -0.58\par  Sing dlr        1.4539         1.4340       -1.37\par  Korean won     1321.60        1259.50       -4.70\par  Rupee            49.04          48.74       -0.61\par  Ringgit         3.4715         3.4500       -0.62\par  Change in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        90.23         111.33      +23.38\par  Yuan            6.8262         7.3041       +7.00\par  Sing dlr        1.4376         1.4382       +0.04\par  Korean won     1254.40         935.70      -25.41\par  Rupee            48.47          39.41      -18.69\par  Rupiah        10900.00        9390.00      -13.85\par  Peso             47.52          41.28      -13.13\par  Baht             34.80          33.68       -3.22\par  Ringgit         3.4650         3.3050       -4.62\par  Taiwan dlr      32.802         32.443       -1.09\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Kevin Yao; Editing by Kazunori Takada)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090105e5120006a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
