\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan to up  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  swaps with S.Korea to $30 bln-media\b0\par\par\uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2008\par 05:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the financial crisis click [ID:nCRISIS]) (Adds comments, details)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 11 (Reuters) - Japan plans to expand its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap lines with South Korea, which has been hit by sharp falls in the won, to about $30 billion from the current $13 billion, Japanese business daily Nikkei reported on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Shoichi Nakagawa said Tokyo is negotiating with Seoul to reach an agreement during a meeting of leaders from the two countries and China on Saturday in the southern Japanese city of Dazaifu.\par \par \uc2 "We are aiming to obtain good results at the summit meeting," Nakagawa said at a parliamentary committee.\par \par \uc2 But neither the minister nor senior officials from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  elaborated on the amount.\par \par \uc2 The reported step would be in line with an agreement by finance ministers from Japan, China and South Korea to consider expanding the size of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap arrangements between their countries. [ID:nN14458729]\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> has been depreciating amid worries about the effect of fallout from the global recession on its export-driven economy, and Seoul has been keen to boost a regional web of bilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap deals, called the Chiang Mai Initiative (CMI).\par \par \uc2 Currently, Tokyo has two types of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap lines with Seoul -- dollar-won and yen-won swap arrangements -- in line with the CMI.\par \par \uc2 Under those arrangements, Japan can provide U.S. dollars worth up to $10 billion and Japanese yen worth up $3 billion to South Korea.\par \par \uc2 In theory, one possibility that may arise from the use of the yen-won swap pact is that South Korea could sell the yen it borrows from Japan to buy dollars in the market, and then use those dollars to conduct won-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Data last week showed that \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves fell again by $11.7 billion to $200.5 billion in November, the lowest level in almost four years.\par \par \uc2 Bahk Byoung-won, South Korea's senior presidential secretary, said last week that South Korean authorities would make only make marginal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market due to a rapid depletion of the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. [ID:nSEO278114]\par \par \uc2 China is also negotiating with South Korea to expand their yuan-won swap facility, and they are expected to officially approve new details during the summit. (Reporting by Yoko Nishikawa and Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/JAPAN-KOREA (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081211e4cb000ho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
