\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-South Korea c.bank rushes to give banks more help\b0\par\par\uc2 855 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2008\par 08:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hold emergency meeting, to boost liquidity\par \par \uc2 * Presidential adviser sees no value in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Presidential adviser accepts 2009 growth to hit decade low\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in November drop to near four-year low (Recasts with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcement)\par \par \uc2 By Cheon Jong-woo and Seo Eun-kyung\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Dec 3 (Reuters) - South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  promised at an emergency meeting on Wednesday to pump more funds into its cash-short financial system, as a top official accepted the economy is heading for much lower-than-expected growth in 2009.\par \par \uc2 The president's top economic adviser concurred with increasingly gloomy economic forecasts that growth in 2009 would be its weakest since the Asian financial crisis a decade ago and said it was pointless to squander the country's falling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to try to lift the weak won.\par \par \uc2 The comments followed news that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves dropped to a near 4-year low in November, increasing pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do more to help lift Asia's fourth-biggest economy as it confronts a global crisis that has already tipped some of its top export customers, including the United States, Japan and the euro zone, into recession.\par \par \uc2 "The presidential secretary is trying to pressure the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more drastic action by highlighting a bleak economic outlook," said Kong Dong-rak, a fixed-income analyst at Hana Daetoo Securities.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee agreed to provide additional support for financial institutions, including paying interest on local bank cash reserves totalling about 500 billion won ($341.8 million).\par \par \uc2 It will also purchase more bonds from local banks and actively supply liquidity to stabilise markets.\par \par \uc2 "These measures are aimed at reinforcing banks' lending capability," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 But it made no mention of interest rates, which it has cut three times since the global crisis took a turn for the worse in September with the collapse of Lehman Brothers investment bank.\par \par \uc2 Still, financial markets expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut its base rate, currently a two-year low of 4.0 percent after cuts amounting to 1.25 percentage point since Oct. 9, at its next monthly rate review on Dec. 11.\par \par \uc2 WEAKER GROWTH\par \par \uc2 "Overall, there is a consensus forecast for growth of some 2-3 percent (next year). I do not disagree with that," senior presidential economic secretary, Bahk Byoung-won, told reporters.\par \par \uc2 The government is expected later this month to revise its 4 percent economic growth forecast for next year. GDP growth this year is expected to be 4.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Growth of 2-3 percent would be the lowest since 1998, when the economy contracted 6.9 percent at the height of the Asian financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 In fact, some investment banks have predicted the economy will shrink in 2009 as much as 3 percent as the global recession drains export demand, long the country's main growth driver, and as domestic demand wilts.\par \par \uc2 Exports last month fell more than 18 percent, their biggest drop in seven years, with one official saying the economy was slowing more than expected.\par \par \uc2 Reserves last month dropped $11.7 billion, hitting their lowest in almost four years at $200.5 billion and following on from a record $27.4 billion decline in October.\par \par \uc2 NOT ENOUGH TO HELP\par \par \uc2 Even though Korea's reserves were the world's sixth largest at the end of October, Bahk said that they were not big enough to lift the won which has lost nearly 40 percent of its value this year against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not have enough capacity for massive \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We will carry out smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when necessary," presidential adviser Bahk said.\par \par \uc2 Since the Asian financial crisis, Asia countries have built up massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to protect themselves against capital flight. Asian reserves are around $4 trillion.\par \par \uc2 But confidence in the cash pile has been severely tested by the global crisis. Worry that the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves might dip below $200 billion have been fanning investor concerns over a fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis.\par \par \uc2 The government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been forced to pump foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the financial system after banks suddenly found themselves unable to raise dollars through normal banking channels as global credit conditions tightened.\par \par \uc2 South Korean banks have looked especially vulnerable to the crisis partly due to their high level of short-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans used to fund the country's exporters.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities steadily injected foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity to ease uncertainty in the local foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  money market due to a persistent global credit crunch," the Bank of Korea said.\par \par \uc2 Officials said they did not expect reserves to fall much further, a prediction echoed by a number of analysts, given an improvement in the current account, a $30 billion dollar credit line at the U.S. Federal Reserves and liquidity injections by other central banks. (Writing by Jonathan Thatcher; Editing By Keiron Henderson)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/KOREA (WRAPUP 2, PIX)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081203e4c3000mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
