\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-South Korean slowdown raises pressure for rate cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cheon Jong-woo and Rhee So-eui   \par \uc2 820 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2008\par 10:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts with market close)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Oct 24 (Reuters) - South Korean growth hit a four-year low and stocks ended their worst week on record on fears that a global recession will hit its export-dependent manufacturers, adding pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent more damage by cutting rates.\par \par \uc2 The main index for Seoul shares plunged more than 20 percent for the week to its lowest close since May 2005 with investors pricing in a sharp drop-off in demand for goods as major overseas markets slip toward recession.\par \par \uc2 Underlining the problems facing Asia's fourth-biggest economy, its largest firm \uc2 Samsung Electronics Co Ltd\uc2  posted a 44 percent drop in third-quarter profit and forecast tough times ahead as consumers around the world feel the pinch.\par \par \uc2 "Oh, don't even bring up that stocks thing," said Son Kyung-hwan, an South Korean insurance agent and investor. "I am never going to dabble in the stock market again in my life. I even had to pay taxes to sell my stocks."\par \par \uc2 The government has announced a raft of measures this week to try to restore investor confidence and steady the wobbling economy, including providing $130 billion to dollar-starved banks and $4 billion to debt-laden builders.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added another just as the stock market closed, saying it would inject emergency liquidity of 2 trillion won ($1.4 billion) via 28-day repurchase agreements.\par \par \uc2 The Korean Composite Stock Index ended down 10.6 percent, dipping below the 1,000 mark for the first time since May 2005.\par \par \uc2 The economic downturn cast a cloud over investors in the streets of Seoul.\par \par \uc2 "Stocks? What do you expect, I'm doomed," said Seol Jae-woo, an education industry worker and investor.\par \par \uc2 SAMSUNG WOES\par \par \uc2 Samsung, which fell almost 14 percent to its lowest since December 2004, and other exporters weighed on the index despite a bounce on Wall Street on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Samsung is facing a lengthy downturn in the once-reliable memory chip market, a rapid margin deterioration in the flat-screen sector and slowing demand for consumer electronics.\par \par \uc2 "We foresee the coming months to be an even more challenging period," said Chu Woo-sik, Samsung's executive vice president in charge of investor relations.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's economy expanded a seasonally adjusted 0.6 percent in the third quarter from the previous three months -- the slowest pace in four years. Facing the headwinds of the global financial crisis, economic prospects are grim.\par \par \uc2 "GDP growth will slow going forward. It is important for the Bank of Korea to not only cut rates but to show a strong commitment to support the economy and join international stimulus effort," said Kim Jae-hong, a Shinyoung Securities economist.\par \par \uc2 UNLIKELY TO MEET FORECAST\par \par \uc2 South Korea was unlikely to meet the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growth forecast of 4.6 percent in 2008 after it grew by 5.0 percent in 2007, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll this week forecast 2008 growth of 4.3 percent and 2009 growth of 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 The economy grew 3.9 percent over a year earlier in the third quarter, sharply down from 4.8 percent in the second quarter and marking its weakest annual performance since mid-2005.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the Bank of Korea cut its benchmark interest rate by 25 basis points to 5 percent, the first reduction since late 2004. The cut was announced a day after key central banks joined forces in trimming rates to try to restore investor confidence to financial markets.\par \par \uc2 A battered won down about 17 percent over the past month and more than a third so far this year, was likely to face more pressure from a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut. It ended local trade down almost 4 percent against the dollar, though suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut some of the losses.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's presidential Blue House said a three-way summit that includes Japan and China is set for mid-December to coordinate plans to boost the regional economy.\par \par \uc2 President Lee Myung-bak, at an Asian-Europe summit in Beijing, has said he also wants to form a new global agency to manage the credit crunch.\par \par \uc2 East Asian leaders said in Beijing they will move to form an $80 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap scheme by the the middle of next year to help cushion the crisis' blow on economies.\par \par \uc2 Lee has warned the global crisis threatened to be more severe than the Asian financial meltdown a decade ago that almost derailed South Korea's economy. At that time, the country was bailed out of its troubles by a rescue package led by the International Monetary Fund worth close to $60 billion.\par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 4, PIX, TV)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081027e4ao000sw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
