\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won down, jittery over Wall Street outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2008\par 02:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Sept 24 (Reuters) - The won fell against the dollar on Wednesday, as doubts about an early stabilisation of Wall Street markets persuaded international traders to keep away from riskier assets such as the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  But the won managed to cut losses, with support from gains in local stock prices on the country's move to tighten supervision on short-selling and after local authorities pledged to help ease shortages in dollar funding in the local swap market.\par \par \uc2  The won <KRW=> ended local trade at 1,154.0/4.6 per dollar, down from Tuesday's domestic close <KRW=KFTC> of 1,149.0 but above a session low of 1,157.9 set earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2  "The market changed little for most of the day after the won's drop at the start of the session due to the volatile U.S. markets, but basis swaps narrowed in the afternoon after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities," said a foreign bank dealer.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials said separately that they would inject sufficient liquidity into the local swap market, which has been struggling with shortages in dollar funding. [ID:nSEO166746] [ID:nSEO134879]\par \par \uc2  Dollar/won basis swaps, which show how much additional costs investors have to pay to secure dollars, narrowed slightly to around -310 basis points from around -360 basis points on Tuesday, dealers said.\par \par \uc2  South Korean financial institutions have been struggling to secure short-term dollar funding as the prolonged turbulence in the U.S. financial sector has raised risk aversion among international traders.\par \par \uc2  The Seoul stock market's benchmark KOSPI <.KS11> index rose 1 percent, as foreign investors were net buyers of 542 billion won worth of local stocks.\par \par \uc2  Regulatory officials have recently said they would draw up measures to tighten supervision on short-selling of stocks in the local market.\par \par \uc2  The Financial Supervisory Service (FSS) said after trading ended that it had discovered many institutional investors had violated the ban on uncovered short-selling, in which investors sell shares that they do not actually own.\par \par \uc2  The FSS said in a statement that it would recommend the Financial Services Commission, the policy-making agency that governs it, tighten regulatory guidelines.\par \par \uc2  South Korea allows only covered short-selling, in which investors can sell borrowed stocks in the hope of buying them back at a lower price later to make a profit.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       0600 GMT     0325 GMT   prev close\par  Won <KRW=>           1,154.0/4.6  1,152.9/3.4      1,149.0\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>    10.8755/22   10.9205/92   10.8941/72\par  KOSPI <.KS11>           1,495.98     1,495.29     1,481.37\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  (Reporting by Yoo Choonsik; editing by Jonathan Hopfner)\par \par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080927e49o00033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
