\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 3- \highlight22\uc2 South Korea intervenes \highlight\uc2 , moves to speed won rebound\b0\par\par\uc2 965 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2008\par 05:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Won rallies after traders report government dollar sales\par \par \uc2 * Korea to scrap ceiling on foreign buying in offshore market\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  predicts modest growth slowdown (Adds fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remarks, updates markets)\par \par \uc2 By Yoo Choonsik and Jonathan Thatcher\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Sept 4 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the battered won for a second day on Thursday, helping give the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  its biggest boost since early July just before it began its tumble to four year-lows on fears of capital flight.\par \par \uc2 An official said South Korea would also relax rules to help accelerate any recovery in the won which has lost 17 percent on the dollar this year and nearly 6 percent in a four-day sell-off triggered by worries foreign holders of about $7 billion of bonds maturing next week would take their money out of South Korea.\par \par \uc2 The selling turned to a frenzy on Tuesday when the Bank of Korea and finance ministry stood by and allowed the won to slide to its lowest level in four years instead of springing to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's defence as they have done for much of the year.\par \par \uc2 The won is Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year and has slumped as the economy, heavily reliant on imports of commodities, felt the full brunt of soaring prices for raw materials and food.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  began rebounding on Wednesday after traders reported the first dollar sales from authorities in more than a week, and extended gains on Thursday after another bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted.\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> was quoted up 1.8 percent at 1,128.0 per dollar at 0714 GMT, its biggest daily percentage gain since early July, up from around 1,148 earlier in the day. Wednesday's domestic close was 1,148.5 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 NO SYSTEMIC RISKS\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers declined to estimate how much authorities had spent on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Lee Seong-tae, also declined to comment in parliament when asked about the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry is in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually conducts the buying or selling.\par \par \uc2 Analysts, including those polled by Reuters on Wednesday, forecast more won weakness this year, although many said comparisons to the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis were overblown.\par \par \uc2 "While we retain the view that fundamentals are aligned for won weakness, speculative fervour surrounding recent moves appear stretched," \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  said in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 In addition, analysts said the $7 billion in bonds that mature next week did not pose a risk to the country's financial system, even if foreign holders repatriate the proceeds.\par \par \uc2 In fact, foreign investors turned net buyers of domestic bonds in August after being sellers the previous two months, data from the country's main securities industry association show.\par \par \uc2 "All the data shows that we have no systemic risk in the financial sector," Rhee Chang-yong, deputy chairman of the Financial Services Commission, told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Governor Lee dismissed talk of crisis, saying South Korea was not confronting problems more serious than those facing many other countries.\par \par \uc2 In a report to parliament, he said sluggish domestic demand will drag on the economy but strong exports will keep the slowdown modest while inflation should start to ease by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 The current account, which has slipped into deficit as commodities prices soared, should also improve with the help of falling raw materials prices.\par \par \uc2 Putting a brave face on the turmoil, the government said it would next week meet investors to sell around $1 billion in government bonds, its first sovereign offering in two years.\par \par \uc2 Still, the mere mention of a capital flight from the bond market has touched a nerve in South Korea, stirring memories of the financial crisis a decade ago when foreign investors dumped assets across Asia.\par \par \uc2 ANCHOR OF STABILITY\par \par \uc2 Its timing could not have been worse either. South Korea is about to run its first annual current account deficit since that crisis. Foreign selling of Korean stocks is at a record this year.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters on Wednesday expect the won to fall another 4.3 percent before recovering in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Goohoon Kwon said the won may be rising under its own steam rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding holders of dollars appear to have already taken profits against the won while there is no further large dollar demand from Korean corporates.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are the world's sixth largest at $243 billion and have served as an anchor of financial stability since the Asian financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Authorities have spent an estimated $30 billion this year to support the won, but moved to the sidelines last month, firing market speculation they were unwilling or unable to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Instead, authorities tried to talk up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They plan to scrap the ceiling on the amount of dollar/won non-deliverable forward sales each bank can take from customers, an official from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities told Reuters on Thursday, speaking on the customary condition of anonymity.\par \par \uc2 The move would buy little in the way of immediate support for the won, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "Foreign appetite to be short dollar/won NDFs is already low, so allowing them to do so does not change the overall sentiment, which remains to be long dollar/won," said Yen Ping Ho, a strategist at \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 . (Additional reporting Vidya Ranganathan in Singapore and Rafael Nam in Hong Kong, Writing by Dayan Candappa, Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-MARKETS (WRAPUP 3)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|G|RNP|PGE|PCM|PEN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080904e49400035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
