\par\par \b \uc2 South Korean won rises on stocks, exporter deals\b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2008\par 02:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to domestic close)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Aug 6 (Reuters) - The South Korean won rose against the dollar on Wednesday as domestic stocks jumped after the U.S. Federal Reserve signalled it would not rush to raise interest rates, and was helped further by exporter demand for settlements.\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> also found support from softer oil prices and as investors remained wary of possible dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities.\par \par \uc2 However, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to rise further as foreign investors kept selling Seoul shares and concerns persist over a slowdown in Asia's fourth-largest economy amid higher inflation, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "Despite various bullish factors, the won's gains were limited. I do not expect U.S. stocks to rise further due to lingering worries about the economy. That will push down shares here and the won," said Lee Tark-koo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at KB Futures Co Ltd.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean unit was quoted at 1,015.7/6.0 per dollar as of 0600 GMT, compared to Tuesday's local close of 1,017.9 <KRW=KFTC>.\par \par \uc2 Seoul shares <.KS11> ended up 2.81 percent after the Fed held U.S. interest rates steady on Tuesday, expressing concerns about both economic growth and inflation and indicating it was in no rush to push borrowing costs higher.\par \par \uc2 But foreign investors sold a net 42 billion won worth of shares in South Korea's main exchange after unloading a combined net 784.3 billion won during the previous three consecutive sessions.\par \par \uc2 Investors are keeping an eye on the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy meeting on Thursday, when the Bank of Korea is expected to raise interest rates to a 7-\u189\'00\'BD year high to tackle inflation.\par \par \uc2 Oil fell further on Wednesday in Asia to stay around a three-month low as concerns over a weak U.S. economy continued to weigh on demand.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       0600 GMT       0240 GMT     prev close\par  Won <KRW=>           1,015.7/6.0    1,016.5/6.9        1,017.9\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>     9.3743/96      9.3764/27      9.4110/73\par  KOSPI <.KS11>           1,578.71       1,567.04       1,535.54\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Cheon Jong-woo; Editing by Jonathan Hopfner)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080809e4860000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
