\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-S.Korea sells dollars after biggest fall in reserves\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cheon Jong-woo   \par \uc2 784 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2008\par 02:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , more analysts, agencies, markets)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Aug 4 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves suffered their biggest-ever monthly drop in July, when the authorities reportedly spent some $15 billion propping up the won, using the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as their main tool to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the authorities sold an estimated $300 million on Monday to further support the won, demonstrating a preference for a firmer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the sharp fall in reserves.\par \par \uc2 Asia's fourth-largest economy is expected to keep bolstering the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <KRW=> to tackle imported inflation, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to be as strong as in July due to weaker oil prices and a slowing economy, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "As oil prices fell from July, the burden of inflation on the government has been eased," said Kim Jae-eun, an economist at Hana Daetoo Securities. "The authorities will not reverse the market trend of a weaker won although they will slow down the speed of the unit's fall."\par \par \uc2 Oil prices <CLc1> hovered just below $126 a barrel on Monday, much lower than the record high of $147.27 in July.\par \par \uc2 Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was small compared with money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July, when the authorities spent an estimated $5 billion on one day itself to support the ailing local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Before local financial markets opened on Monday, the Bank of Korea said the country's foreign reserves fell $10.58 billion to $247.52 billion by the end of July from a month earlier, citing "efforts aimed at stabilising the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market".\par \par \uc2 It was the biggest monthly decline since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started compiling foreign reserves figures in 1971.\par \par \uc2 The sharp fall in reserves heightened concerns South Korea could be confronted with an economic crisis similar to the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis, and raised expectations the authorities might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less, losing their grip on the won.\par \par \uc2 But senior officials at two of the world's top credit rating agencies told Reuters that there was no problem with the country's external payment capability despite the drop. [ID:nSEO256291]\par \par \uc2 NO CHANGE IN \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  POLICY\par \par \uc2 A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  official also told Reuters the authorities saw no reason to change their policy of defending the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as one of the main tools to contain inflation.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities will consider falls in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves when we manage \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, but there is no reason for falls to change the policies," said the official, who declined to be name.\par \par \uc2 He added that concerns over the drop in reserves had been misplaced.\par \par \uc2 The remarks, along with actual dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helped the won limit its falls, but could not prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from shedding 0.26 percent against the dollar during the local session.\par \par \uc2 Analysts agreed with the rating agencies' assessment, saying South Korea's overall external payment capability remained strong.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities have sufficient ammunition. The reserves' fall in July was smaller than expected, which indicates they have not only the reserves but also other means for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Lee Tark-koo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at KB Futures Co Ltd.\par \par \uc2 July's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped lift the won by almost 4 percent against the dollar for the month. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen 11 percent for the January-June period.\par \par \uc2 The authorities were also seen reducing their long-positions on dollar non-deliverable forwards (NDFs) and using dollar sell and buy swaps; selling dollar spots on certain days and later buying dollar futures.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not provide the exact number of dollars it and the Finance Ministry sold during the month as they tried to prevent the sliding won from exacerbating high inflation, which has been led by imported commodities.\par \par \uc2 "We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market last month to correct mounting speculative trades, but the market usually overstates the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  size," a Bank of Korea official, who declined to be named, said by telephone without giving the exact value.\par \par \uc2 But it was unusual for the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which also attributed some of the decline to losses in dollar terms from non-dollar assets, to admit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Consumer-price inflation climbed to a near 10-year high of 5.9 percent in July, up from 5.5 percent in June.\par \par \uc2 South Korean authorities are worried accelerating inflation will cut the disposable income of consumers at a time when a slump in domestic demand is expected to dent growth this year. (Additional reporting by Yoo Choonsik and Lee Shin-hyung, Editing by Jonathan Hopfner and Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/RESERVES (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080804e4840000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
