\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-S.Korea in high-stakes policy game with won battle\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan and Yoo Choonsik   \par \uc2 974 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2008\par 10:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE/SEOUL, July 11 (Reuters) - For all their talk of free markets, the South Korean authorities left investors in no doubt this week about who should control their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the won.\par \par \uc2 Using 5 percent of Korea's huge pile of foreign reserves, the Bank of Korea relentlessly pounded markets, selling dollars and pushing the won up 5 percent to what it regards as an appropriate level to fight imported inflation, near 1,000 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 By Friday, the bears had backed off and the authorities appeared to have won the first battle hands down.\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=KFTC> was steady, just on the weaker side of 1,000 per dollar at 1,002.20/30.\par \par \uc2 "Perhaps the range on dollar/won is 980-1,025 multi-week rather than 1,015-1,060," said Westpac Bank's Sean Callow, the latter range being what investors had previously expected.\par \par \uc2 But analysts are worried. By using force to strengthen the won and deferring interest rate rises, the authorities may find themselves caught in a prolonged war as they fight against deteriorating economic fundamentals, a loss of confidence in the economy and damage to their inflation-fighting credibility.\par \par \uc2 An immediate risk is that they will have to keep expending their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to defend that line in the sand until all the speculators concede defeat.\par \par \uc2 ING economist Tim Condon said that if South Koreans began to doubt the authorities could win, they would hedge against their exposure. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation then becomes self-reinforcing."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  can easily afford the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the world's sixth-largest stockpile of reserves at $258 billion.\par \par \uc2 What it probably cannot afford is the damage this policy gamble does to its reputation as an inflation-fighting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  committed to liberal markets.\par \par \uc2 "They can hold the fort for the third quarter," said \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  strategist Claudio Piron.\par \par \uc2 "But the won \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could build problems later on as the credibility gap widens," he added, interpreting comments by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor after a policy meeting on Thursday as being dovish on interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "It seems like the targeting of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate may conflict with the Bank of Korea's mandate to establish price stability with interest rate policy."\par \par \uc2 NO OPTION?\par \par \uc2 But what if the Bank of Korea was left with no choice but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the won?\par \par \uc2 Market participants are not discounting the possibility that Korea's presidential Blue House has seized the monetary policy reins, eager to shore up the economy after conceding growth would probably be far lower than the lofty levels promised by President Lee Myung-bak before his landslide election victory in December.\par \par \uc2 This week the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its policy rate unchanged for the eleventh month in a row, even though annual inflation at 5.5 percent is above the 5 percent base rate.\par \par \uc2 Placing the onus entirely on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy fails, the country could face an economic crisis, although the chances are not big," said Lee Tark-koo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Kookmin Futures.\par \par \uc2 "The won will probably drop sharply when the authorities lose the game; that will boost inflation and interest rates, and in turn prompt foreign investors to sell local bonds, which again will push up interest rates and the debt burden on households."\par \par \uc2 In the longer term, the won will remain under pressure from a rising oil import bill and a falling stock market.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's current account could be in deficit in 2008 for the first time in 11 years and the country could soon be a net borrower from external markets for the first time in eight years.\par \par \uc2 Short-term external debt stood at $176.5 billion, or 67 percent of the total \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, at the end of March, the highest ratio since the end of March 1999.\par \par \uc2 Raising interest rates carries its own risks. Besides hurting borrowers, it could spur businesses to access cheaper foreign money, potentially setting the country up for short-term debt problems of the kind that triggered the 1997/98 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 A rate rise could rein in bank lending, which is expanding at a worrying pace of over 15 percent, far higher than the nominal economic growth rate. At the same time, a bigger interest burden could spell danger for over-leveraged banks and consumers, and risk a downgrade for the A-rated economy.\par \par \uc2 KEEPING FOREIGNERS SWEET\par \par \uc2 ING's Condon believes investor confidence is as important a factor as fundamentals in deciding the won's trajectory.\par \par \uc2 Having changed tack on their earlier preference for a weak won to help exporters, South Korea's game plan now, he reckons, is to ensure the economy remains attractive to foreigners.\par \par \uc2 Economic growth and strong corporate earnings then become as important as price stability in terms of policy objectives, which makes a strong won and steady interest rates an ideal policy mix.\par \par \uc2 "Korea is a nation of risk takers," said Condon, reckoning the country had the capacity to carry huge debt and run a current account deficit. "The pressure on the authorities is to maintain an attractive environment for investment." So far, the plan is working, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have steadied the won, which at its weakest point this month had shed 10 percent of its value against the dollar in just six months.\par \par \uc2 "At least, Korea's current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is consistent with keeping a tight monetary policy, whereas in 2005-2007 they were trying to weaken the won even as they hiked rates," said \uc2 Westpac\uc2 's Callow. (For a factbox on recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  click [ID:nSEO7888]) (Editing by Alan Raybould)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-WON/ (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|ABX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080714e47b000kq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
