\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX- \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  over past year\b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2008\par 10:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * For a related story, click on [ID:nSP340095]\par \par \uc2 July 11 (Reuters) - The South Korean authorities have sold almost $20 billion -- the size of Iceland's economy -- in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets over the past three-and-a-half months to prop up the won and fight imported inflation, dealers estimate.\par \par \uc2 South Korea has removed almost all \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls since the 1997-98 Asia financial crisis but it has frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, sometimes by issuing warnings, sometimes by directly trading \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months marks a shift in its policy stance from that of the past 10 years, when it was determined to ensure the won <KRW=> <KRW=KFTC> did not rise too fast against the dollar because a strong won hurts exports.\par \par \uc2 The shift in policy came as inflation rose to its highest in nearly a decade in June due to the rising cost of importing energy and raw materials.\par \par \uc2 Following are key dates in the past year when dealers have reported the authorities buying or selling dollars against the won, or when policy makers issued remarks aimed at influencing the exchange rate, as compiled by Reuters:\par \par \uc2 July 11, 2008 - Won rises slightly, authorities seen selling a small amount of dollars.\par \par \uc2 July 10, 2008 - Won rises slightly, authorities seen selling a small amount of dollars. One-month won offshore forward contracts rise overnight and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cited.\par \par \uc2 July 9, 2008 - Won hits its highest in over two months; authorities seen selling about $5 billion -- a record amount for one day -- and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials issue multiple warnings of measures to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 July 8, 2008 - Won hits a two-week high; authorities seen selling about $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 July 7, 2008 - Finance ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  say in a joint statement they will take aggressive, concerted action and sell dollars when necessary to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 July 2, 2008 - Won soars 2 percent from a session low; authorities seen selling $3 billion; a finance ministry official warns of action to stabilise the market.\par \par \uc2 June 2008 - Authorities seen selling about $5 billion in total during the month, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on at least five days.\par \par \uc2 May 2008 - Authorities seen selling about $3 billion in May, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on at least two days.\par \par \uc2 April 2008 - Authorities seen buying a small amount of dollars.\par \par \uc2 March 2008 - Authorities seen selling about $1 billion on March 18 to support the won, but buying about $800 million on March 26 to send the won down.\par \par \uc2 October 2007 - Authorities spotted buying more than $1 billion before and after the won rises to 10-year highs on at least four days.\par \par \uc2 July 2007 - Authorities seen buying more than $1.5 billion on at least four days to stem the won's rise. (Writing by Yoo Choonsik; Editing by Alan Raybould)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-WON/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|ABX|RNP|PCM|PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080711e47b000li\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\pi