\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-S&P sceptical S.Korea  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  will work\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik   \par \uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2008\par 16:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, July 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision this week to step up dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the won was probably politically motivated, and its effect is very questionable, an S&P official told Reuters on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 South Korea had better raise interest rates as a means to contain inflation because the dollar-selling policy could only provide traders with chances to increase their sales of the won <KRW=>, he said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "It's impossible to resist the market force for a long period of time, because the size of the international money, either for speculation or investment, is much much bigger than the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves can withstand," said Takahira Ogawa, director of sovereign ratings at \uc2 Standard & Poor's.\par \par \uc2 The comments came hours after dealers reported the South Korean authorities had sold up to an estimated $2 billion to prop up the won to cushion imported inflation. The won ended local trade up 1 percent on the day on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "The result of (the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), the effectiveness of that is very questionable," Singapore-based Ogawa said while visiting Seoul for a seminar.\par \par \uc2 Analysts were also sceptical South Korea, which had already drawn some $10 billion this year on its nearly $260 billion foreign reserves to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had slim chances of winning the game against the market.\par \par \uc2 "Both the government and the Bank of Korea need to be very careful because if they send a wrong signal to the market, then (investors) may take this as an advantage to sell the Korean won further because the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are buying," Ogawa said.\par \par \uc2 The won had fallen 10 percent against the dollar this year until Monday, dragged down by expectations that the country's current account will swing into the first annual deficit in 11 years and by the increasing demand for dollars to buy oil.\par \par \uc2 The weakening won has contributed significantly to pushing up the country's annual consumer-price inflation to a near 10-year high of 5.5 percent in June.\par \par \uc2 "I suspect this (was) a politically motivated decision. Politics is irrational. So in one sense, even knowing the negative implication, they may have to choose this," he said.\par \par \uc2 When asked whether \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  should have raised interest rates instead of using the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, he agreed: "I think (that's) more orthodox."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea has held the policy interest rate steady at 5.0 percent for the past 10 months in the face of conflicting risks from high inflation and slow economic growth, and is widely expected to keep the rate unchanged again on Thursday. (Editing by Gerrard Raven)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/ (INTERVIEW, URGENT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|ABX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080711e4780019a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
