\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,177 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2008\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SOUTH KOREA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WON SURGES ON SUSPECTED GOVERNMENT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The won rose sharply after traders said the South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in both the domestic market Tuesday and the offshore non-deliverable market overnight to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0150 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN BUSINESS CONDITIONS DN 7 PTS TO NIL IN JUNE -NAB \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian business conditions deteriorated sharply and unexpectedly in June, further confirming a sharp economic slowdown as high interest rates, record fuel prices and an ongoing credit squeeze put a stranglehold on activity. (published 0130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =3RDUPDATE:GRIM NZ BUSINESS CONFIDENCE PUTS JUL EASING IN PLAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's business confidence remained at a 34-year low in the second quarter, confirming recessionary economic conditions which leaves the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  poised to embark on rate cuts, possibly as early as this month. (published 0440 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: 5-YR JGB AUCTION STRONG AS SENTIMENT IMPROVES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand was strong at an auction of five-year Japanese government bonds Tuesday, as faltering share prices and receding expectations for an interest rate hike boosted the bonds' attractiveness. (published 0535 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: JAPAN JUN BANK LENDING GROWTH FASTEST IN 2 YRS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lending by Japanese banks grew at its fastest pace in almost two years in June on increased corporate fund demand, while growth in money supply accelerated. (published 0217 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN JUNE 1-20 TRADE DEFICIT Y230.43 BILLION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan posted a merchandise trade deficit of Y230.43 billion in the first 20 days of June, compared with a Y448.83 surplus in the year-earlier period, the Finance Ministry said Tuesday. (published 0010 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN JUNE CORP BANKRUPTCY CASES UP 8.1% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese corporate bankruptcies rose 8.1% in June to 1065 cases, private credit research agency Teikoku Databank Ltd. said Tuesday. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA ECONOMY MAY GROW BELOW 6.2% TARGET FOR 2008-FIN MIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indonesian economy may grow below an initial target of 6.2% this year because of to high inflation, Finance Minister Sri Mulyani Indrawati told reporters Tuesday. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S&P: S KOREA ECONOMY TO GROW 4.3% THIS YEAR VS +5.0% IN 2007 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A senior analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services said Tuesday he forecasts South Korean economic growth of 4.3% this year, lower than the 5.0% expansion recorded in 2007. (published 0547 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA AIMS TO UP 2012 HANDSET EXPORTS TO $70B VS $29B '07 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea is targeting to raise its exports of mobile phones to $70 billion in 2012 from $29 billion in 2007, the Ministry of Knowledge Economy said Tuesday. (published 0215 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par G8 EXPRESSES "STRONG CONCERN" ABOUT HIGHER OIL, FOOD PRICES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Leaders from the Group of Eight leading industrialized nations Tuesday expressed "strong concern" about threats being posed by rising oil and food prices to the world's economy. (published 0544 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA INDUSTRY TRADE GRP URGES GOVT TO MANAGE CNY RISE-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An industry trade group urged China to "flexibly manage" the pace of yuan appreciation and boost export-tax rebates on exports of certain technologically advanced goods, such as consumer electronics, the Commerce Ministry-backed International Business Daily reported Tuesday. (published 0230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA TO CONTINUE USING TARIFFS TO STEADY PRICES - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China will continue its policy of using tariffs to increase the supply of goods and services and stabilize prices, the China Financial and Economic News reported Tuesday, citing an unnamed official. (published 0203 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MALAYSIA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO SELL MYR3.5B FEB 2018 MGS JULY 14 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plans to auction MYR3.5 billion of February 2018 Malaysian Government Securities, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said on its Web site Tuesday. (published 0323 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ST GEORGE BANK\f28 \uc2 \f2  TO SELL A$506M ABS - MARKET SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 St. George Bank Ltd.\f28 \uc2 \f2  plans to sell A$506 million in asset-backed securities, a market participant who had seen the term sheet said Tuesday. (published 0447 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TELECOM CORP OF NEW ZEALAND\f28 \uc2 \f2  PRICES CHF200M SWISS BOND ISSUE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Telecom Corp. of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Tuesday it has priced a CHF200 million Swiss bond issue, the proceeds from which will be used to refinance debt and general corporate purposes. (published 0348 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OCBC TO UNDERWRITE S$500M IN BONDS FOR SINGAPORE HDB -SOURCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's Housing and Development Board has hired \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp. Ltd.\f28 \uc2 \f2  to underwrite S$500 million in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds, two people familiar with the matter said Tuesday. (published 0507 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par COMMODITIES BOOM TO INSULATE AUSTRALIAN ECONOMY -BIS SHRAPNEL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The global commodities boom will likely help insulate the Australian economy from recession over the next five years, although commodities prices are expected to trough in either 2010 or 2011, a report from BIS Shrapnel released Tuesday said. (published 0050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA MINIMUM WAGE RISE POSES THREAT TO ECONOMY - ACCI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Fair Pay Commission Tuesday agreed to raise the minimum wage by over 4% per week, prompting a warning from industry that the move could trigger an economy-wide price breakout as firms look to claw back higher wage costs. (published 0515 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINE FIN SECY: SCRAPPING VAT ON OIL TO COST PHP73.1B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philippine Finance Secretary Margarito Teves Tuesday warned the government stands to lose PHP73.1 billion should lawmakers scrap the value-added tax on oil. (published 0219 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM JUNE NEW VEHICLE SALES UP 60% ON YR BUT FALL ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sales of new vehicles in Vietnam surged 60% on year in June, rising for the 23rd straight month. (published 0256 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA PM TO PUSH FOR PROGRESS ON FOOD, OIL SECURITY AT G8 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd said Tuesday he will push for progress on the issues of food and energy security and climate change at a Group of Eight outreach summit in Japan Wednesday. (published 0233 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMANY'S MERKEL: "VERY SATISFIED" WITH G8 EFFORTS - KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Chancellor Angela Merkel expressed her strong satisfaction Tuesday with Group of Eight efforts to combat global warming, as well as soaring food and crude oil prices, Kyodo News reported. (published 0202 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: MONGOLIA TOGROG SET TO RISE MORE AS UNREST WINDS DN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Mongolian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  looks set to continue, or even accelerate, its steady climb against the U.S. dollar, driven by efforts to curb local inflation and as the recent wave of political violence winds down. (published 0510 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: CHINA JUN EXPORT, MONEY-SUPPLY GROWTH LIKELY SLOWED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese exports likely rose more slowly in June because weakening global economic growth hurt demand for China-made goods, economists said. (published 0358 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: KOREA'S INFLATION FIGHT STILL LACKS CONVICTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean authorities want us to think they are serious about their pledge to fight inflation and therefore support the won. Indeed, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has gained a fair bit in the past two days with the U.S. dollar down as low as KRW1,026, in part due to a burst of rhetoric and market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by government officials. (published 0127 GMT) [ 08-07-08 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080708e47800074\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pi