\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2008\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SOUTH KOREA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WON SURGES ON SUSPECTED GOVERNMENT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The won rose sharply after traders said the South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in both the domestic market Tuesday and the offshore non-deliverable market overnight to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0150 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN BUSINESS CONDITIONS DN 7 PTS TO NIL IN JUNE -NAB \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian business conditions deteriorated sharply and unexpectedly in June, further confirming a sharp economic slowdown as high interest rates, record fuel prices and an ongoing credit squeeze put a stranglehold on activity. (published 0130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: NZ BUSINESS CONFIDENCE VERY GRIM: JULY EASING POSSIBLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's business confidence remained at a 34-year low in the second quarter,confirming recessionary economic conditions which leaves the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  poised to embark on rate cuts, possibly as early as this month. (published 0004 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN JUNE 1-20 TRADE DEFICIT Y230.43 BILLION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan posted a merchandise trade deficit of Y230.43 billion in the first 20 days of June, compared with a Y448.83 surplus in the year-earlier period, the Finance Ministry said Tuesday. (published 0010 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par COMMODITIES BOOM TO INSULATE AUSTRALIAN ECONOMY -BIS SHRAPNEL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The global commodities boom will likely help insulate the Australian economy from recession over the next five years, although commodities prices are expected to trough in either 2010 or 2011, a report from BIS Shrapnel released Tuesday said. (published 0050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FED'S YELLEN HINTS INFLATION MAY BE RISING CONCERN FOR FED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco President Janet Yellen on Monday signaled that the the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be more hawkish in fighting inflation in the future. (published 1739 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FED'S YELLEN: CREDIT CONDITIONS TIGHTER DESPITE RATE CUT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco President Janet Yellen on Monday said that credit conditions are tighter despite recent rate cuts. (published 1628 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US FED, SEC SIGN INFORMATION-SHARING AGREEMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Federal Reserve and Securities and Exchange Commission on Monday signed a formal agreement aimed at boosting information sharing and cooperation between the agencies. (published 1937 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EU'S JUNCKER: EURO ZONE MINS DIDN'T CRITICIZE ECB HIKE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance ministers from the 15 countries that use the euro didn't use a meeting with European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet to criticize last week's rate hike, Luxembourg Finance Minister and Prime Minister Jean-Claude Juncker said Monday. (published 2044 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: KOREA'S INFLATION FIGHT STILL LACKS CONVICTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean authorities want us to think they are serious about their pledge to fight inflation and therefore support the won. Indeed, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has gained a fair bit in the past two days with the U.S. dollar down as low as KRW1,026, in part due to a burst of rhetoric and market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by government officials. (published 0127 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VIEW: SOUTH KOREAN WON LOOKING LESS LIKE A LOSER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean won has been losing ground, but that may be changing now that the country's government, armed to the hilt with foreign reserves, is showing a determination to defend its embattled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 1936 GMT) [ 08-07-08 0200GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080708e4780002h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
