\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-S.Korea May c/a deficit dents inflation fight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lee Jin-joo and Yoo Choonsik   \par \uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2008\par 01:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds value of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in paragraphs 3, 12, 13)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, June 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account swung to a deficit in May on the back of soaring oil prices, the third monthly gap this year which could undermine the already weak local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and add to inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's current account produced a seasonally adjusted $1 billion deficit in May after a revised $2.73 billion surplus in April, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> <KRW=KFTC> fell more than 1 percent to a 1-month low of 1,048.0 per dollar as record high oil prices heralded more current account pressure, before bouncing back sharply when authorities sold $1 billion to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts said South Korea's current account in the rest of the year would largely be shaped by global prices for oil and other raw materials, almost all of which are imported by Asia's fourth-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 "Our view is that the current account will show a surplus for the second half of the year, but this is on the assumption that oil prices don't rise much from the current level," said Lim Ji-won, an economist at \uc2 JPMorgan Chase.\par \par \uc2 In the first five months of the year, South Korea recorded a seasonally adjusted current account deficit of $1.89 billion, compared with a $2.74 billion surplus for the same period in 2007, the Bank of Korea said.\par \par \uc2 For a graphic on the current account figures, click here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://customers.reuters.com/d/graphics/KR_CURAC0608.gif"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://customers.reuters.com/d/graphics/KR_CURAC0608.gif}}}\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told reporters almost all of the $7.17 billion unadjusted January-May current account deficit was a result of soaring oil prices.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil <CLc1> has jumped 46 percent this year, reaching a record above $140 a barrel on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 REGULAR \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The current account figures added to the selling pressure on the won, which has lost about 10 percent so far this year against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The won's weakness, which exacerbates price pressures by making imports more expensive, comes at a time when the country is already grappling with high inflation led by skyrocketing commodities prices.\par \par \uc2 The won ended local trade down half a percent on the day at 1,041.5 per dollar, having briefly recovered to positive territory after the reported dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , estimated by three dealers at up to $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 Friday marks the third day of reported dollar selling to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by South Korean authorities in less than two weeks, selling as much as $3 billion over the period,\par \par \uc2 according to estimates by dealers.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea forecast late last year that the country would post a current account deficit of $3 billion this year for the first time in 11 years, but has recently said the shortfall could be bigger due to higher oil prices.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's young government under President Lee Myung-bak has recently shifted its economic policy goal from lifting economic growth sharply above market expectations to fighting inflation, which is running at a seven-year high of 4.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 The finance ministry has said it expects annual inflation to pass the 5 percent mark this month.\par \par \uc2 Friday's data also showed that South Korea's overseas borrowing fell by a net $4.77 billion in May after a net drop of $0.18 billion in April.\par \par \uc2 International credit rating agencies have expressed concerns about a jump in South Korea's short-term external debt in recent years, led by banks to fund robust lending and dollar forward deals at home.\par \par \uc2 NEWS LINKS * [ID:nSP298685] for FACTBOX on inflation confronting Asia * [KR-MCE-RTRS] for South Korean macroeconomic stories * [KR/ECI] for latest South Korean economic data * <ECONKR> for live South Korean economic data (Editing by Keiron Henderson and Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/CURRENT (UPDATE 4)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080626e46q0038v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
