\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  won drops as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  worries ease\b0\par\par\uc2 356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2008\par 05:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to noon)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, June 18 (Reuters) - The South Korean won retreated from a near two-week high against the dollar on Wednesday, on speculation that recent government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at supporting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have run its course.\par \par \uc2 The government was seen selling some $1 billion on Tuesday after unloading an unknown amount of dollars on Monday to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of a bid to tame inflation.\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> was quoted at 1,022.1/5 against the dollar, down from its previous closing bid of 1,018.5.\par \par \uc2 It briefly rose to 1,018.2 in early trade, marking its strongest level since 1,016.75 on June 5.\par \par \uc2 Dealers continued to push the dollar lower, wary of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities. But the won's rebound in the past three consecutive sessions fanned speculation that the government would likely cease to step in.\par \par \uc2 "For the authorities, which have said that sharp movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate are undesirable, it would be burdensome to pull the dollar down further," a local bank dealer said.\par \par \uc2 "Unless the dollar turns sharply higher, we will not see as much heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as yesterday and the day before."\par \par \uc2 Analysts said inflation worries might continue to exert downward pressure on the won, with South Korea's consumer price inflation hitting a near seven-year high of 4.9 percent in May.\par \par \uc2 The figure far exceeded the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range of 2.5-3.5 percent for the sixth month in a row.\par \par \uc2 Seoul shares <.KS11> edged up 0.3 percent, with foreign investors selling a net 42.5 billion won by midday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        0308 GMT    0059 GMT     prev close\par  Won <KRW=>              1,022.1/5   1,019.1/6    1,018.5/9.0\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>     9.4683/737  9.4427/500     9.4314/387\par  KOSPI <.KS11>            1,756.28    1,757.91       1,750.71\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Kim Yeon-hee; Editing by Jonathan Hopfner)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080621e46i00029\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
