\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-S.Korea leader warns of resource crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik   \par \uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2008\par 03:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts with producer inflation, details, markets)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, June 10 (Reuters) - South Korea's president warned on Tuesday that the country faced a "resource crisis", comments underlined by data showing that producer inflation shot to it highest level since the Asia financial crisis a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Bond prices fell and the won rose as financial markets saw the comments and data as raising pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten monetary policy when it reviews interest rates on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The firmer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also reflected the market view that authorities might allow the won to appreciate to offset the impact of rising imports costs as raw materials prices shoot higher globally.\par \par \uc2 "International crude oil prices have doubled over a year and grains and raw materials prices are also rising sharply. Accordingly, our economy is faced with a serious difficulty, with prices rising and the economy gradually slowing," President Lee Myung-bak said.\par \par \uc2 "We are faced with a 'resources crisis' coming next only to the oil crisis in the 1970s and the financial crisis in the 1990s," he said in a speech at a ceremony to mark the 21st anniversary of a pro-democracy protest rally.\par \par \uc2 After his comments, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its producer price index in May rose 11.6 percent over a year earlier, the highest annual growth since an 11.7 percent gain in October 1998 and up sharply from a 9.7 percent gain in April.\par \par \uc2 That suggested more price pressure in the pipeline for consumer inflation, already running at a 7-year high close to 5 percent, in Asia's fourth-biggest economy.\par \par \uc2 The data came shortly after Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would resist rising inflation, which weighed on bond prices globally as investors factored in the chance of a U.S. rate rise. [ID:nN09303720]\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea holds a policy meeting on Thursday. It has held its key rate steady at 5.0 percent for the past nine consecutive months, after raising it by a combined 1.75 percentage points since October 2005.\par \par \uc2 CABINET OFFERS TO RESIGN\par \par \uc2 June treasury bond futures <KTBc1> tumbled to 5 month lows. Futures fell as much as 43 ticks to 105.67, the lowest since the then front-end contract hit 105.51 on Jan. 10.\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> rose to 1,021 per dollar from Monday's domestic close of 1,031.25, partly helped by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Stocks <.KS11> tumbled 2 percent.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  relies almost wholly on imports of most energy, food and raw materials supplies, making it especially vulnerable to current skyrocketing prices around the world.\par \par \uc2 A labour union representing 13,000 truckers and transport workers voted on Monday to strike over high oil prices, threatening to halt much of the transportation of export cargoes to ports.\par \par \uc2 The vote came despite a government announcement on Sunday of a $10.2 billion package to help the poor, small businesses and truckers to cope with rising energy bills.\par \par \uc2 Lee, who swept to office on a high economic growth policy, also faces mounting pressure over his handling of opposition to sensitive beef imports from the United States.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Han Seung-soo and the other members of the country's three months-old cabinet offered to resign early on Tuesday after massive street protests sparked by an unpopular deal to open the market wider to U.S. beef imports.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's annual consumer price inflation shot up to a 7-year high of 4.9 percent in May, and the finance ministry is widely expected to lower this year's economic growth target, set at 6 percent, next month.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea's chief said last month the country's economy would be lucky if it could achieve growth of 4.5 percent, having expanded 5 percent last year and 5.1 percent in 2006.\par \par \uc2 President Lee won December's election with a pledge to lift economic growth sharply but has seen his approval rating dive to 20 percent after his first 100 days in office. (Additional reporting by Rhee So-eui, Editing by Keiron Henderson and Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 2, PIX)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080610e46a0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
