\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-S.Korea spends nearly $2 bln to prop up ailing won\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lee Shin-hyung and Cheon Jong-woo   \par \uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2008\par 04:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts with fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , economist, markets)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, May 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times to support the sliding won on Tuesday and warned the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have fallen too far, as it wrestled with the problem of containing inflation without choking off economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea is under political pressure to cut rates to help spur growth in Asia's fourth-largest economy, and minutes from an April policy review showed two of the six board members favoured a rate cut for the first time since November 2004.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is resisting that pressure because inflation is at a four-year high, partly due to a surge in oil prices and won weakness, which is making imports more expensive.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Asia's second-worst performer this year, may have fallen too far, Choi Jong-ku, head of the finance ministry's international bureau, told Reuters as news of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  began surfacing and pushed the won to a one-week high.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were seen selling almost $1 billion on Tuesday afternoon for won after dumping up to $800 million during the morning session, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The won's rise on Tuesday revived expectations among investors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have more room to cut interest rates at its next review on June 12.\par \par \uc2 Others said the Bank of Korea, hemmed in between rising inflation and the wishes of a growth-hungry new government, would want to avoid cutting rates for fear of stoking inflation.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Korea's governor implied at his post-meeting comments in May that as long as the won turns weaker, it would be impossible for an interest rate cut in the near future," said Park Jong-yeon, fixed-income analyst at Woori Investment.\par \par \uc2 Annual inflation at 4.1 percent in April was above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2.5 percent to 3.5 percent target range for a fifth straight month.\par \par \uc2 "The won fell as oil prices jumped but we are worried that the won's fall might be excessive," Choi told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 He also said the government was worried the won's fall was due to excessive herd behaviour in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday marks the second attempt to shore up the won in under a week. That pushed up the won <KRW=> to a session high of 1,034.9, matching the high on May 19, which was the strongest since May 8.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean authorities spent an estimated $500 million last Wednesday when the won weakened past 1,050 per dollar to a 2-1/2-year low.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's June Treasury bond futures <KTBc1> ended 21 ticks higher on Tuesday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  MINUTES\par \par \uc2 Some board members of the Bank of Korea had said at the April rate meeting that a weaker won had more of a negative than positive impact on macroeconomic management, minutes of the meeting showed, reducing the case for a rate cut.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicates the government is more concerned about inflation than growth but it still likely wants a relatively weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support exports to help meet an ambitious economic growth target of 6 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 The government has repeatedly backed a weaker won to help boost growth in the export-focused economy, but analysts say fears that it will also lift inflation at home means the authorities will likely step in to prevent any nosedive.\par \par \uc2 "The comment (by Choi) comes as the government needs to show action as a weaker won boosts the impact of surging oil prices on domestic demand," said Jeong My-young, an analyst at Samsung Futures Inc.\par \par \uc2 "But the authorities will never allow the won to strengthen past the 1,000 (per dollar) again," she added.\par \par \uc2 Inflation -- which touched a four-year high of 4.1 percent in April -- has been a major factor keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from cutting interest rates. (Additional reporting by Lee Soo-jung and Lee Jin-joo; Editing by Keiron Henderson and Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/WON (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080527e45r0002i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
