\par\par \b \uc2 South Korea won extends falls on oil, weak shares\b0\par\par\uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2008\par 03:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to midday)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, May 26 (Reuters) - The South Korean won extended its falls against the dollar on Monday as oil prices pushed back up and as local shares fell amid foreign investors' mounting sales.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official's comments late last week that exports from Asia's fourth-largest economy would probably miss expectations also discouraged investors from buying the won.\par \par \uc2 But the local unit found some relief as investors went back on alert for possible dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  approached 1,050 won/dollar, a level which triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <KRW=> was quoted at 1,048.6/9.2 per dollar as of 0315 GMT, compared to its previous closing bid of 1,045.5.\par \par \uc2 "A fall in local shares caused investors to prefer dollar holdings but everybody was cautious as the won approached to a level where the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a local bank dealer.\par \par \uc2 Last Wednesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were reported by dealers to have sold an estimated $500 million as the won weakened past 1,050/dollar to hit its lowest in more than two and a half years.\par \par \uc2 Oil rose towards $133 a barrel on Monday in Asia on lingering concerns over supply and on a weaker dollar, edging back towards last week's record level above $135.\par \par \uc2 South Korean shares <.KS11> dropped 1.92 percent as foreign investors sold a net 186 billion won worth of shares in the country's main exchange.\par \par \uc2 Kim Byung-hwa, a deputy governor at South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Friday the country's exports this year would probably miss expectations despite the won's recent weakening, which has helped offset the impact of slowing demand. [ID:nSEO313988]\par \par \uc2 On the corporate front, Daewoo Shipbuilding and Marine Engineering Co Ltd said late on Friday it had won a 1.44 trillion won order to build two drill ships from the Americas, fuelling demand for the won.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       0315 GMT      0040 GMT    prev close\par   Won <KRW=>          1,048.6/9.2   1,048.2/8.6       1,045.5\par   Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>   10.1530/17    10.1492/99       10.1161\par   KOSPI <.KS11>          1,792.77      1,801.05      1,827.94\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Cheon Jong-woo; Editing by Keiron Henderson)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080529e45q0001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
