\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-S.Korea props up won as economic problems pile up\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lee Shin-hyung and Lee Soo-jung   \par \uc2 806 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2008\par 03:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with more details, markets closing)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, May 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the won on Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped to a 2-1/2-year low as the government grappled with rising foreign debt, accelerating inflation and slowing economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two months followed news that the government was looking to check foreign borrowing that has doubled the country's external debt since 2005. Any curbs would shrink the supply of dollars converted to won.\par \par \uc2 The won is already Asia's second worst peforming \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year and a Bank of Korea official was quoted as saying that further declines would fuel inflation, running above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target.\par \par \uc2 Tim Condon, head of Asia research at ING, said in a note that the flurry of comments about the rapid build-up of external debt might be due to worries about a possible credit rating downgrade.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Moody's\uc2  sovereign analyst was recently in Korea for the annual review and we think the agency signalled the obvious, namely that its A2 sovereign rating could be jeopardised if the growth of short-term external debt does not slow," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The won rose as much as 1 percent to 1,039.5 per dollar <KRW=> after purchases that dealers believed were made on behalf of the authorities. It earlier hit a low of 1,057.2, the weakest since October 2005, against Tuesday's close of 1,050.3.\par \par \uc2 "A sharp fall in the won lifts the risk of higher inflation while it does not create many jobs," the unnamed Bank of Korea official told online media outlet NewsPim.\par \par \uc2 The government, which is pressing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to raise interest rates, may have to cut its target for economic growth this year, a finance ministry official said.\par \par \uc2 "We have consumption, investment and employment indicators showing the economy is entering a slowing phase," Yim Jong-yong, head of the ministry's economic policy bureau, told reporters after a scheduled meeting on the financial market situation.\par \par \uc2 "(The government) may revise this year's economic growth and consumer price growth forecasts when it announces economic policy direction in the second half."\par \par \uc2 As current account and inflation problems grow, officials have already said it would be hard for the government to achieve its 6 percent economic growth target, far above a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast of 4.7 percent and dismissed as unrealistic by analysts.\par \par \uc2 Local financial markets are suffering, with the won falling 3.7 percent against the dollar this month, Dubai crude prices in won terms rising 13 percent and the benchmark five-year treasury bond yield <KR5YT=KSDA> up half a point.\par \par \uc2 Five dealers estimated the authorities sold $500 million to $700 million to prop up the won on Wednesday. Two dealers had initially placed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at about $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 BONDS RATTLED\par \par \uc2 Government bond prices fell despite the warnings on slower growth. June bond futures <KTBc1> fell 20 ticks to 106.87.\par \par \uc2 Oil price increases and the weakness in the won could push up inflation further -- it hit a four-year high of 4.1 percent in April -- and prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from cutting interest rates soon, or even force it to raise them.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, bond investors are worried about possible government measures to curb overseas borrowing by banks, which could prompt banks to unload their bond holdings to obtain cash. Converting that into dollars could further depress the won.\par \par \uc2 Vice Finance Minister Choi Joong-kyung reaffirmed that his ministry was studying ways to reduce overseas borrowing but had not yet decided on a course of action.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's overseas liabilities more than doubled to $381 billion at the end of 2007 from $188 billion two years before.\par \par \uc2 Banks have been borrowing to fund forward deals with local exporters, to swap dollars for won and lend the proceeds on, or to invest in higher-yielding Korean bonds.\par \par \uc2 The interest rate gap encourages such borrowing. A bank could borrow one-year dollars on Wednesday and swap them into won at an effective rate of just 2.5 percent. That compares with Korean interbank rates <KIKRW> of 5.57 percent.\par \par \uc2 Overseas borrowing, if unchecked, could dent the country's balance of payments and hurt its credit ratings. \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  Ratings Service warned of the problem in October last year.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the government had few options, other than to issue warnings to banks or limit the amount each bank can borrow. (Additional reporting by Cheon Jong-woo; Writing by Yoo Choonsik; Editing by Jonathan Hopfner and Alan Raybould)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 3) (attn choonsik)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|ABX|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080521e45l0002c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
