\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  won up 1 pct,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  reported   \b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 01:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, March 18 (Reuters) - The South Korean won rose 1 percent against the dollar on Tuesday as the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were believed to have sold $1 billion to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to tackle higher inflation.   \par \par \uc2 Market players stayed wary of possible dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the won seen unlikely to stay firm as investors remained worried about the global credit crunch and a slowdown in the U.S. economy, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 "It is questionable if the authorities will take strong actions further as the recent fall in the won is not because of speculation but fundamentals," said a foreign bank dealer.   \par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> was last quoted at 1,013.9/4.3 per dollar after rising as much as 1.3 percent, compared with its prior closing bid of 1,023.9.   \par \par \uc2 South Korea's President Lee Myung-bak is concerned about the won's sharp decline, a spokesman said, after a deputy finance minister said the authorities planned to take steps against the sharp falls in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nSEO263407]   \par \par \uc2 With those comments, the authorities sold dollars, mostly during the morning session. The reported dollar purchases marked the first such major \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at propping up the won since early 2002.   \par \par \uc2 A weaker won usually bolsters import prices, which results in higher inflation and slower domestic demand, especially when prices of oil and other raw materials are high.   \par \par \uc2 The South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen 7.7 percent against the dollar so far this year while the won <JPYKRW=R> has been down almost 20 percent versus the yen.   \par \par \uc2 Seoul shares <.KS11> closed up 0.91 percent while foreign investors sold a net 407 billion won worth of shares in South Korea's main exchange.   \par \par \uc2 Investors are keeping an eye on the result of the Federal Reserve's rate setting meeting later in the day and a gathering of South Korea's senior officials on Wednesday to check financial markets.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to slash interest rates by as much as a whole percentage point at its policy meeting as investors warily await investment bank results which could aggravate fears of a full-blown market crisis.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                          0742 GMT     0310 GMT   prev close\par  Won <KRW=>              1,013.9/4.3  1,014.4/4.8      1,023.9\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>      10.3968/020  10.4513/608      10.5069\par  KOSPI <.KS11>              1,588.75     1,574.04     1,574.44\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Cheon Jong-woo and Lee Soo-jung; Editing by Jacqueline Wong)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080321e43i0001g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
