\par\par \b \uc2 Korea Won Ends Down As BOK Further Defends KRW900/Dlr Level   \b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2007\par 09:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Close    Change \par  USD/KRW            903.60   +2.90 \par  JPY/KRW            7.8349   -0.0212 \par  3-Year Treasurys   5.46%    +3 bps \par  5-Year Treasurys   5.52%    +3 bps \par  10-Year Treasurys  5.59%    +3 bps \par  20-Year Treasurys  5.65%    +3 bps \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won closed lower against the U.S. dollar Thursday following suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and fund repatriation by offshore investors looking to profit from the won's recent gains, market participants said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Shortly after the market opened, traders said the Bank of Korea may have bought about $100 million to $200 million at the day's low of KRW900.40 via unknown foreign banks in a determined move to keep the dollar above KRW900.00.   \par \par \uc2 "The BOK has bought dollars in small amounts in order to increase the breathing space from the key defense line at the KRW900 level," a local bank trader said.   \par \par \uc2 In addition, some foreign investors repatriated funds to take "profits from the strong won," said a second local bank trader.   \par \par \uc2 For Friday, traders expect the won to consolidate with players taking a breather after the market's recent high volatility.   \par \par \uc2 The authorities appeared to try talk up the won the day after the U.S. Federal Reserve cut its policy interest rate by a quarter percentage point Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the Fed isn't likely to cut interest rates again next month and dollar weakness is unlikely to persist.   \par \par \uc2 In addition, Minister of Finance and Economy Kwon Okyu said the government is concerned that market players are betting too heavily that the dollar will continue to depreciate against the won.   \par \par \uc2 "If players become more and more aware of the low chance of additional rate cuts in the U.S., they may give up their strong dollar-selling bias of late," said a foreign bank trader.   \par \par \uc2 Still, "the dollar's global weakness is not exactly over yet," another foreign bank trader said, adding that local exporters will take advantage of the dollar's rebound.   \par \par \uc2 Traders tip the dollar to trade between KRW900.00 and KRW907.00 Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Government bonds closed lower, mirroring the fall in Treasury prices overnight in the U.S.   \par \par \uc2 The Fed had emphasized its concerns over inflation in a statement accompanying its rate decision, and many players took this as a hint that further U.S. rate cuts may not be in the offing.   \par \par \uc2 Looking forward, bond yields may rise further given a likely increase in issuance of monetary stabilization bonds to combat the high liquidity caused by BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's consumer price index in October rose 3.0% on year, higher than market expectations for a 2.8% rise.   \par \par \uc2 "The figures suggest that oil prices will definitely be a key issue for some time to come," said a local securities firm trader.   \par \par \uc2 In addition, the Ministry of Commerce, Industry and Energy said exports in October jumped 24.2% on year to $34.79 billion.   \par \par \uc2 "Considering the string of positive trade, industrial production and service sector data, the Korean economy has perhaps upgraded to the 'expansion phase' from the 'recovery phase,'" said Lee Sang-Jae, an economist at  \uc2 Hyundai Securities.\par \par \uc2 The three-year yield is tipped to move in a 5.44%-5.49% band, and the benchmark five-year yield in a 5.50%-5.55% band Friday.   \par \par \uc2 -By Ga-Woon Vahn, contributing to Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; djnews@seouldowjones.com [ 01-11-07 0800GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1063 1858   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071101e3b1000kq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
