\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Korea Oct Exports Jump;BOK Suspected Buying USD Again   \b0\par\par\uc2 925 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2007\par 06:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail on trade and inflation data, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .)   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--South Korean exports rebounded in October after September's drop but the government warned that the strong won could hurt overseas sales in coming months.   \par \par \uc2 Along with most other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the South Korean won has been rising against the U.S. dollar of late, prompting suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Korea again Thursday to keep the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above KRW900.   \par \par \uc2 Traders estimate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $100 million to $200 million Thursday after three suspected rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday totaling around $700 million.   \par \par \uc2 Last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exports jumped 24.2% from a year earlier to a total $34.79 billion while imports increased by 27.2% to $32.59 billion, the Ministry of Commerce, Industry and Energy said Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Growth in both was faster than economists had anticipated, although most had forecast a jump because the three-day Chusok holiday, which fell in September this year, pushed down that month's exports and imports.   \par \par \uc2 The end of the Chusok holiday also helped to stem price rises with the October consumer price index up a smaller 0.2% on month, although it was up a sharper 3.0% on year. In September, prices were up 0.6% on month and 2.3% on year.   \par \par \uc2 The impact of the strong won will likely start to show in export figures for December, a senior official at the Ministry of Commerce, Industry and Energy said following the release of the data.   \par \par \uc2 At 0500 GMT, the dollar was at KRW902.50 after closing Wednesday at KRW900.70, its lowest closing level since KRW900.50 on Aug. 26, 1997.   \par \par \uc2 "Some say that the current \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  level is endurable, while others say that companies are suffering greatly, but (exporters) will likely see answers from December," said Oh Jung-Kyu, director general for Trade & Investment Promotion at the commerce ministry.   \par \par \uc2 But he indicated that exports would likely maintain double-digit growth into the early part of 2008. Double-digit growth figures are usually taken to mean growth of between 10% and 20%.   \par \par \uc2 The commerce ministry said it will meet with a group of exporting companies later this month to discuss their difficulties in coping with the strong won.   \par \par \uc2 Minister of Finance and Economy Kwon Okyu though was quick to calm politicians about the potential damage of a strong won, telling a parliamentary audit Thursday that the government expects local companies to cope with the current exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 Ahn Byung Chan, a director-general of the Bank of Korea's international department also told Dow Jones Newswires that the won is expected to stabilize against the U.S. dollar since the U.S. Federal Reserve was unlikely to cut interest rates next month.   \par \par \uc2 Exports On Slowing Trend   \par \par \uc2 Although exports showed a big recovery in October, the government noted the trend of overseas sales was a touch slower than earlier in the year.   \par \par \uc2 "Exports averaged 11.3% growth on year to $32.09 billion in September and October put together while imports averaged a 11.9% rise to $29.81 billion, which is slower than the performance shown in the earlier part of this year," the ministry said.   \par \par \uc2 Exports increased in October because of a rise in demand for general machinery, wireless communications devices, automobile, petrochemical products and liquid display devices, the ministry said.   \par \par \uc2 On the other hand, semiconductors, steels and ships and vessels, other mainstay export items, contracted on year.   \par \par \uc2 "We should keep a close watch on the drop in semiconductor and steel exports, which was due to continued falls in chip prices and the rise in domestic steel demand," the ministry said.   \par \par \uc2 Oh said steel producers may be turning to the local market because the won's appreciation cuts into the profits from selling overseas.   \par \par \uc2 Imports rose due to a surge in international crude oil prices and a recovery in domestic consumption, according to the data.   \par \par \uc2 The National Statistical Office attributed the on-year rise in consumer prices to costly oil and higher vegetable prices. Analysts had forecast October consumer prices to rise 2.8% on year.   \par \par \uc2 Trade Surplus Shrinks A Touch   \par \par \uc2 The trade surplus was $2.20 billion in October, narrowing slightly from September's $2.35 billion and $2.39 billion a year earlier, the preliminary data showed.   \par \par \uc2 Economists had forecast the trade surplus at $1.82 billion on the back of exports rising 21.8% and imports increasing 26.0%. In September, exports fell 0.9% to $29.38 billion and imports dropped 2.2% to $27.03 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Economic data in September and October tend to differ drastically, depending on the timing of the Chusok holiday. The holiday fell in the last week of September this year and in the first week of October last year.   \par \par \uc2 "The exports result (in October) is due to a 3.5-day increase in working days because the Chusok holiday was in October last year," the ministry said in a statement.   \par \par \uc2 By region, exports to the U.S. rose 32.6% on year in the first 20 days of October, a turnaround from a 9.2% fall in the same period last year, because of strong demand for liquid display devices, wireless communications equipment and automobiles.   \par \par \uc2 Exports to China jumped 33.1% on year and sales to the E.U. rose 23.7%.   \par \par \uc2 -By Jin-Young Yook, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; jin-young.yook@dowjones.com [ 01-11-07 0521GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071101e3b1000e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
