\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-S.Korea's  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  too costly for the economy   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik   \par \uc2 864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2007\par 05:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Aug 7 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's effort to restrain a rising won looks like an exercise in futility: It's expensive, unlikely to work, and not needed to support exports.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities, buying dollars and flooding the market with won, also contradicts efforts to control inflation caused by a rise in the money supply, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 Politicians want to appease manufacturers who worry a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurts export competitiveness, but the reality is that South Korea's exports are booming and the won will rise to where the market thinks it should be.   \par \par \uc2 "The won overvalued? Yes, if we had the KOSPI at 1,500 and if the world economy was so bad that the Fed cut interest rates twice," said Oh Suk-tae, an economist at  \uc2 Citigroup.\par \par \uc2 The Seoul stock market's benchmark index <.KS11> has recently roared past 2,000, before retreating 8 percent, and the world economy is faring better than many expected earlier in the year.   \par \par \uc2 Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a global sell-off in riskier assets combined to push the won <KRW=> sharply below an almost 10-year high and away from what officials say is an overvalued level. That's unlikely to last.   \par \par \uc2 The country's rise in exports -- up 20 percent last month -- rising interest rates, firm economic growth and a bright stock market all point to a higher won, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 "They (Finance Ministry officials) may feel happy now with the won's recent drop, but everyone knows it's a temporary turbulence and the won will rise again. Exports are really strong and ship makers continue to win orders," Oh said.   \par \par \uc2 On July 25, it was trading just 0.2 won away from a decade high against the dollar of 912.5, when the authorities stepped in with heavy dollar purchases, sending it into a 1 percent reverse.   \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, it traded around 923 won to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 CONTRADICTORY POLICY   \par \par \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's fifth-largest, expanded by $4.1 billion in July to $254.8 billion. Dealers estimate the Bank of Korea sold more than $2 billion worth of won during the last few days of the month.   \par \par \uc2 In its desperation to keep a lid on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday reinstated a curb on foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lending to domestic customers, abolished six years ago, even though the move runs counter to a pledge to liberalise capital flows.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says its fears are not just the impact on exports -- which generate about a quarter of GDP -- but that if the won rises too high, the inevitable tumble back down could wreak havoc on its financial markets and push up inflation.   \par \par \uc2 Rhee Gwang-ju, who led the Bank of Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy until his promotion as a deputy head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  early this year, has repeatedly warned of the risk.   \par \par \uc2 In an interview with Reuters in May, he said of the won's continued rise: "It is on the path of overshooting and as time goes on, a correction will take place. We are concerned about a sharp depreciation of the won in a short period of time later this year."   \par \par \uc2 The won lost half its value at the height of the 1997 Asian financial crisis and is still almost 9 percent below its level at the end of 1996.   \par \par \uc2 IRONY   \par \par \uc2 Exports have been growing six years in a row and in the four years to 2006 doubled in value, despite the won's appreciation.   \par \par \uc2 "This is testament to the fact that Korean industries have rapidly moved up the value-added chain, reminiscent of what Japan went through in the 1980s spurred by a strengthening ... (yen),"  \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist, Mark Tan, wrote in a research report.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts also point to the paradox of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumping money into the markets by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market just as it pledges its determination to curb growth in money supply.   \par \par \uc2 "The main economic risk from continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the need to absorb excess liquidity. Already, the authorities have to issue large amounts of Monetary Stabilisation Bonds (MSB) to siphon off liquidity," said Frederic Neumann, an economist at  \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Markets based in Hong Kong.   \par \par \uc2 He was referring to bonds that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells to drain surplus money from the financial system. The outstanding amount of MSBs on issue had jumped 35 percent in 2004 and 9 percent in 2005 before slowing in growth since then.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea raised interest rates by a total of 1.5 percentage points between October 2005 and July this year to cool sizzling money growth, which policy makers are worried will push up asset prices and eventually stoke inflation.   \par \par \uc2 "Money supply growth continues to be strong in Korea, which prompted the authorities recently to raise interest rates even though inflationary pressures remain low. In a sense, therefore, the costs of exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is borne by the overall economy via higher interest rates," he added.   \par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/WON (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070810e3870006f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
