\par\par \b \uc2 South Korea bonds gain on auctions, weaker stocks   \b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2007\par 02:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, July 18 (Reuters) - South Korean government bonds snapped a three-day losing streak to edge up on Wednesday, as a drop in the local stock market buoyed demand for bonds and after a government bond auction produced strong results.   \par \par \uc2 But gains were limited due to uncertainties over the U.S. Treasury debt market's direction and worries the South Korean authorities may sell the won for the dollar to cap the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark five-year treasury bonds <KR5YT=KSDA> fell two basis points to end at 5.48 percent, and the three-year treasury bond yield <KR3YT=KSDA> also lost two basis points to 5.40 percent.   \par \par \uc2 "The stock market's drop today helped revive buying interest in bonds today, while investors welcomed the outcome from the inflation-linked bond auction," said Kim Hyung-won, a fixed-income analyst at  \uc2 Hyundai Securities.\par \par \uc2 The Seoul stock market's benchmark KOSPI <.KS11> slipped 0.96 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry sold only 102 billion won ($111.3 million) out of the planned 200 billion won in inflation-linked 10-year treasury bonds at an auction on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 But investors welcomed the outcome because the auction was oversubscribed and they believed the ministry scaled down the sales amount to keep the issuing yield from rising too much.   \par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> ended local trade up 0.2 percent against the dollar, racing closer to a near-decade high and sparking fears the authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have pledged to keep the won from rising excessively to protect local exporters from a sharp erosion in their price competitiveness in overseas markets and in their profit margins.   \par \par \uc2 When the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars to cap the won's strength, it usually sells bonds to absorb the increased won liquidity afterwards, pressuring the overall trading sentiment in the fixed-income market.   \par \par \uc2 The September treasury bond futures <KTBc1> rose 9 ticks to 106.82.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                        close   prev close\par  5-yr treasury bonds <KSDA02>          5.48 pct     5.50 pct\par  3-yr treasury bonds                   5.40 pct     5.42 pct\par  1-yr monetary stabilisation bonds     5.34 pct     5.35 pct\par  3-mth certificates of deposit         5.08 pct     5.07 pct\par  Average call rate <1SYR=KMBC>         4.72 pct            ~\par  6-mth *KORIBOR <KIKRW=>               5.26 pct     5.25 pct\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ~ not quoted * Korea interbank offered rate ($1=916.6 Won)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-BONDS (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070721e37i0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
