\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -S.Korean won rallies, others lose momentum   \b0\par\par\uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2007\par 09:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 3 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dithered on Tuesday after a four-session rally driven by the dollar's weakness, amid verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fears elsewhere that the gains were overdone.   \par \par \uc2  Still, Korea's won <KRW=> managed to rise 0.4 percent and hit a 7-week high of 916.8 per dollar at one point. The Thai baht <THB=> rose 0.45 percent and the Singapore dollar <SGD=> hit a one-week high near 1.5220 per U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2  China's yuan <CNY=CFXS> also rose to its highest level against the dollar since the July 2005 revaluation, hitting 7.5951 per dollar, giving traders another reason to be bullish on Asia.   \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar has gained more than a percent in the past four sessions.   \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's weakness was broad-based. It matched a 26-year low versus sterling and stayed near a record low against the euro <EUR=>, undermined by the view that the Federal Reserve is unlikely to lift rates anytime soon given a recent run of soft inflation data and concerns over the mortgage market.   \par \par \uc2  But some of the Asians seemed to have lost steam. Heading into the U.S. Independence Day holiday on July 4, traders expected other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also to take a breather.   \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso <PHP=> retreated from a 2-week high of 45.73 to end the day nearly steady. The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> barely budged from near 9,000 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2  "U.S. equities are rallying on the lower rate hike risk and that seems to be the story Asia is running with: no Fed hike so the party is on!," said Sean Callow, Westpac Bank's strategist.   \par \par \uc2  "Personally, I don't think it's sustainable," he said, adding that oil prices near $71 a barrel would start to hurt Asian sentiment at some point.   \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Korean authorities were clearly worried about the won's rise above 920-per dollar this week.   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, which include the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overshooting its fundamental values and they were watching the market. They expressed concern the won was gaining even as foreigners have turned net sellers of Korean equities.   \par \par \uc2  The charts, however, seemed to suggest further upside for the won. The next target for the falling dollar/won pair is the 913-per-dollar level, which was its December 2006 trough, said Winston Tang, an analyst at 4CAST. Tang said the dollar/won downtrend was firmly established after the pair broke through the May 2007 lows near 922.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at   0747 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       122.40         122.63       +0.19\par  Sing dlr        1.5222         1.5259       +0.24\par  Taiwan dlr      32.797         32.765       -0.10\par  Korean won      917.80         921.50       +0.40\par  Baht             34.32          34.46       +0.41\par  Peso             45.77          45.89       +0.26\par  Rupiah         8995.00        9000.00       +0.06\par  Rupee            40.57          40.67       +0.25\par  Ringgit         3.4355         3.4340       -0.04\par  Yuan            7.5953         7.6057       +0.14\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \hi