\par\par \b \uc2 DJ OECD Recommends Korea Steady Policy Rate, Flexible  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Policy   \b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2007\par 12:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--South Korea should focus on achieving its mid-term inflation target while maintaining a flexible exchange rate policy, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 'Monetary conditions are now judged to be tighter than at any time since the 1997 financial crisis, while the pace of economic activity (has) slowed and inflation remained low and stable,' the OECD sad in its economic survey on South Korea.   \par \par \uc2 'Additional monetary tightening appears unnecessary in such an environment.'   \par \par \uc2 It said further hikes in the short-term interest rate could hurt households and small to medium enterprises, two sectors of the economy where debt has been increasing.   \par \par \uc2 The OECD recommended that the Bank of Korea focus its monetary policies on achieving the 2.5%-3.5% medium-term inflation target and avoid using key interest rates as a tool to stabilize real estate prices. The OECD is estimating a 2.5% rise in the consumer price index for 2007 and a 3.0% gain in 2008, which are well within the BOK's target band.   \par \par \uc2 The international organization blamed the BOK's interest rate hikes for the won's appreciation.   \par \par \uc2 'The upward pressure on the won has occurred in the context of a significant hike in interest rates by the Bank of Korea, even though inflation has been below the target zone,' it said.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its policy interest rate by 125 basis points to 4.50% in five rate moves between October 2005 and August 2006.   \par \par \uc2 The Korean won appreciated 2.3% against the U.S. dollar in 2005 and by 8.8% in 2006. However, during the BOK's monetary easing in 2004 to stimulate growth, the won appreciated 15.2% against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The OECD discouraged the BOK from using monetary policy to affect the property market, which was one of the reasons the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates in August 2006.   \par \par \uc2 'Monetary policy is a blunt instrument for influencing real estate prices, particularly as the largest house price increases are limited to specific areas of the capital region,' the OECD said.   \par \par \uc2 It said Korea's increase in the average nationwide house price since 2000 is well below the average of other OECD countries and remained moderate in 2005-2006.   \par \par \uc2 As for the government's housing policies to curb property prices, the OECD said the policies should focus more on developing an efficient market than on controlling prices.   \par \par \uc2 'Despite the merits of the objective of stabilizing housing prices, some of these policies have the potential to create substantial harm if allowed to persist in the long term as they tend to reduce the supply of housing,' the OECD said.   \par \par \uc2 Regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the OECD said \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  should pursue a flexible policy by limiting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2 'The liberalization of controls on capital outflows should aim at improving long-run efficiency rather than moderating upward pressure on the won in the short run,' the OECD said.   \par \par \uc2 The OECD said there was no need for continued accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which totaled $250.74 billion at the end of May, because the reserves are nearly double short-term overseas debt. Data from the Bank of Korea showed short-term overseas debt totaled $129.75 billion at the end of March.   \par \par \uc2 The OECD said Korean exports have grown less sensitive to the appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of the steady improvement in product quality and a shift toward knowledge-based products that tend to be less price-sensitive.   \par \par \uc2 The OECD is forecasting South Korea's gross domestic product growth at 4.3% in 2007 and 4.8% the following year. It estimates Korea's potential growth rate to be around 4.5%, a view shared by the International Monetary Fund.   \par \par \uc2 The 2007 forecast is barely below estimates made by the Bank of Korea and the International Monetary Fund, both of which predict 4.4% growth this year. -By Jin-Young Yook, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; jin-young.yook@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Mary de Wet   \par \par \uc2 -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020070620e36k005eh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
