\par\par \b \uc2 Korea Won Ends Down On Suspected BOK  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ;Bonds Fall   \b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2007\par 10:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Close    Change \par  USD/KRW            934.10   +6.00 \par  JPY/KRW            7.7001   +0.0191 \par  3-Year Treasurys   5.15%    +3 bps \par  5-Year Treasurys   5.19%    +4 bps \par  10-Year Treasurys  5.26%    +3 bps \par  20-Year Treasurys  5.30%    +1 bp \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won closed Friday at its lowest level against the U.S. unit in six weeks, as foreign banks covered shorts actively on suspected hefty dollar buying by the Bank of Korea, market participants said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 "The BOK may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pushed the local pair above KRW934.0, in order to help the yen/won cross rate climb back to its psychologically important KRW7.70 level," said a foreign bank trader.   \par \par \uc2 According to a second foreign bank trader, "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have bought more than yesterday's purchase of around $1 billion," bringing the suspected dollar purchases by the BOK to more than $2 billion over two sessions.   \par \par \uc2 The BOK's suspected buying pushed the daily trading volume to $12.48 billion Friday, the biggest ever daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading volume in the market, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar/won is likely to extend its gains Monday, as many players are expected to favor the dollar-buying stance because "the BOK has clearly proven its intention to support both the dollar/won as well as the yen/won cross rate," said the second foreign bank trader.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said local importers, mainly oil refiners, are likely to assist the pair's upside trend Monday, given their active dollar demand recently.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar is likely to trade between KRW932 and KRW937 Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Government bonds closed lower on active selling after positive U.S. jobs and manufacturing data overnight indicated a stronger U.S. economy, which could provide an added boost to trade-dependent \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy.   \par \par \uc2 In addition, the BOK's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has left the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market flush with won, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to absorb through heavy issues of stabilization bonds in the near term.   \par \par \uc2 On the domestic front, there are no fresh cues nor economic data to reflect on, thus "players are slowly beginning to react to offshore data," said a local securities firm trader.   \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, bond yields are likely to rise further Monday as "players expect the government to sell large sums of stabilization bonds due to massive won outflows recently in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said  \uc2 SK Securities\uc2 ' analyst Kong Dong-Rak.   \par \par \uc2 Middle- and longer-term yields will continue to receive upside pressure as the certificate of deposit rate is rising due to banks' ongoing short-term liquidity shortage.   \par \par \uc2 The three-year yield is tipped to move in a 5.12%-5.19% band, and the benchmark five-year yield in a 5.16%-5.23% band Monday.   \par \par \uc2 -By Ga-Woon Vahn, contributing to Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; djnews@seouldowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 18-05-07 0806GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1063 1858   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070518e35i000il\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
