\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won slips on Fed, warnings on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  movements   \b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2007\par 02:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2  SEOUL, May 10 (Reuters) - The South Korean won <KRW=> fell against the dollar on Thursday as the Federal Reserve expressed concern about inflation, lowering expectations of a rate cut, and a local official warned about the won's excessive movements.   \par \par \uc2  The won also fell on importers' deals and foreign equity investors who had sold Seoul shares in prior sessions, prompting market players to cover dollar-short positions.   \par \par \uc2  But the won's losses were limited by demand from shipbuilders and other exporters and as foreigners turned to net buyers of Seoul shares, which hit a record high.   \par \par \uc2  The won was last quoted at 925.0/5.3 per dollar, compared to its previous closing bid of 923.3.   \par \par \uc2  "Overall conditions, such as importer deals and offshore offers, were unfavourable for the won, and exporters' demand that fell short of expectations put further pressure on the unit," said a foreign bank dealer.   \par \par \uc2  Investors remained cautious over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities to check the won's strength, but authorities were not suspected of buying the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  Kim Sung-jin, deputy finance minister, said the country would closely monitor and take appropriate action on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation and the won's excessive movements. [ID:nSEO273489]   \par \par \uc2  The yen <JPY=> was at 120.06/10 per dollar versus 120.04/06 in late New York trade on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors purchased a net 105.3 billion won worth of shares on Seoul's main stock market after selling 198.4 billion won worth of stocks in the previous session.   \par \par \uc2  The benchmark KOSPI <.KS11> closed up 0.39 percent after rising to a lifetime high of 1,616.06.   \par \par \uc2  On the local economic front, South Korea's top state research agency lowered its forecast for the country's trade surplus to $25.6 billion from a $29.5 billion surplus projected earlier. [ID:nSEW000130]   \par \par \uc2  An official in South Korea's shipbuilding industry told Reuters that he expected the country's shipbuilders to win more orders in 2007 than last year as they secured $12.3 billion in the first quarter, 2.5 percent higher than a year ago.   \par \par \uc2  Market players largely shrugged off the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's widely expected decision to leave interest rates steady at 4.50 percent. [ID:nSEO261169]   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       0705 GMT      0340 GMT    prev close\par  Won <KRW=>             925.0/5.3     924.4/5.1         923.3\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>     7.7038/83   7.6982/7060        7.6910\par  KOSPI <.KS11>           1,599.68      1,610.03      1,593.42\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070513e35a0000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
