\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea won hits 1-mth high; importers limit gains  \b0\par\par\uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 01:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Updates to midday)  \par \par \uc2  0320 GMT - The South Korean won <KRW=> hits a fresh month high against the dollar, helped by speculation that the next rate move by the U.S. Federal Reserve may be a cut and as Seoul shares gain ground.  \par \par \uc2  - But the won gives up some initial gains as importers such as energy companies sell the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for settlements and as major companies are scheduled to pay dividends to foreign stake holders from next week.  \par \par \uc2  - Investors remain wary of possible dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities.  \par \par \uc2  - The won quoted at 936.9/7.7 per dollar, after rising to a session high of 936, the strongest since 936 on Feb 21. That compared to its previous closing bid of 937.6.  \par \par \uc2  - "The South Korean unit is likely to rise further, given a weaker dollar after the Fed's decision, but demand for the won is not overwhelming supply," says a local bank dealer.  \par \par \uc2  - The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged at 5.25 percent and said it remained uneasy about inflation, but dropped an explicit reference to the possibility of taking rates higher, leaving its options open. [ID:nN21416941]  \par \par \uc2  - Most Wall Street analysts see the Fed keeping interest rates on hold in May, but many expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to start lowering rates soon after, according to a Reuters poll. [ID:nWAT007185]  \par \par \uc2  - On Seoul's main stock market, the KOPSPI index <.KS11> up 0.47 percent, helped by the Fed's decision on Wednesday, as foreign investors buy a net 6.3 billion won in shares.  \par \par \uc2  - The yen <JPY=> quoted at 117.49/54 per dollar versus 117.53/55 in late New York trade on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2  - On the local economic front, South Korea's Vice Finance Minister Kim Seok-dong reiterates previous forecasts that Asia's third-largest economy would pick up in the second half after being pressured in the first, and forecast healthy exports in March.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                     0320 GMT     0030 GMT   prev close\par  Won <KRW=>           936.9/7.7    936.2/6.8        937.6\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>   7.9709/97   7.9673/719       7.9721\par  KOSPI <.KS11>         1,449.60     1,451.47     1,442.85\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070325e33m0000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
