\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Of Korea Gov: Korea GDP To Grow Mid-4% In 2007  \b0\par\par\uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2007\par 06:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--Bank of Korea Governor Lee Seongtae said Thursday that the country's gross domestic product will grow around the mid-4% range this year, slower than last year's 5% growth.  \par \par \uc2 "The economy is moderating with private consumption slowing, but given a continued recovery in exports, the economy will show mid-4% growth this year," Lee told the National Assembly's finance and economy committee. [ 22-02-07 0551GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Lee also said exports will remain strong, although he admitted that domestic demand is slowing.  \par \par \uc2 Future monetary policy will be aimed at taming inflation, which is likely to stay stable throughout this year, and at improving the economy, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea said in a statement that it would monitor the country's asset market even more closely this year. Spurred by higher housing costs, asset prices have been rising in South Korea, threatening inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The governor said efforts by the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb property costs appear to be working because the real estate market is showing signs of stabilization.  \par \par \uc2 "Since the rise (in housing prices) is slowing, it seems like the policies are showing effects... but it still needs to be watched continuously," Lee said.  \par \par \uc2 Lee said the record-high \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve level isn't the result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of January, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to $240.23 billion, up $1.27 billion from a month earlier, largely because of capital gains from investments of U.S. dollar holdings.  \par \par \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are the fifth-largest in the world.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jung-Ah Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165;jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Graham Norris and Mary de Wet [ 22-02-07 0639GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070222e32m0005p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
