\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Philippine peso at new high, others range-bound  \b0\par\par\uc2 874 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2007\par 03:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 16 (Reuters) - The Philippine peso extended this week's gains to hit a six-year peak against the U.S. dollar on Friday, while the Singapore dollar drew support from tax changes in the budget.  \par \par \uc2 The yen rallied more than half a percent against the dollar <JPY=>, holding near a one-month high hit the previous day after strong Japanese economic growth data.  \par \par \uc2 This lent some support to Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited gains, while trade was also subdued before next week's Chinese New Year holiday in parts of the region.  \par \par \uc2 Markets in China, Hong Kong, Malaysia, Singapore, South Korea and Taiwan are closed on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The peso <PHP=> strengthened as far as 48.09 to the dollar, its highest level in almost six years. It is Asia's strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year with a gain of 1.8 percent, spurred by strong inflows of remittances from overseas workers and a rally in the local stock market.  \par \par \uc2 "Upbeat regional sentiment is making the peso stronger," said a Manila trader. "There is always the possibility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming in, but I don't think they have been in yet."  \par \par \uc2 Another peso trader said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of capping peso gains at around 48.23/25 per dollar on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers in Singapore and \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also on guard over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  is watching the won's <KRW=> recent sharp rise against the yen <JPYKRW=R> but is not targeting a specific rate, senior finance ministry official Hur Kyung-wook said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The won dipped as much as a quarter of a percent to almost 937 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 UPBEAT SINGAPORE DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2 In Singapore traders remained wary of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore after it was suspected of buying U.S. dollars aggressively in the market earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 But they said sentiment towards the Singapore dollar <SGD=> remained bullish, helped by Thursday's budget.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore government said it would raise the goods and services tax (GST) to 7 percent from 5 percent from July 1 and cut corporate tax by 2 percentage points to 18 percent from next year. This helped send Singapore's Strait Times Index <.STI> to a record high in early trade on Friday before easing.  \par \par \uc2 "The outlook remains very positive -- stocks are doing well, the budget was reasonably good and the corporate tax cut adds to the feel-good factor," said one Singapore dollar trader.  \par \par \uc2 "The MAS is likely to be in the market around the 1.5320 area to buy U.S. dollars, but the overwhelming feeling is to sell the U.S. dollar on rallies."  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar was trading about 1.5330 per U.S. dollar, up about 0.7 percent from a one-month low hit on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Sean Callow, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac Bank, said U.S. capital flows data on Thursday suggested U.S. investors' appetite for foreign assets was growing and this boded well for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The United States reported a net overall capital outflow of $11 billion for December, the first outflow in nearly two years.  \par \par \uc2 "U.S. investors are increasing their demand for equities and Asia is clearly getting a chunk of that," Callow said.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> briefly strengthened to about 9,030 per dollar -- its strongest in just over a month.  \par \par \uc2 There was some focus on comments by Aslim Tadjuddin, a deputy governor at Bank Indonesia, who said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees room for a cut next month in its reference interest rate.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia has cut its key rate nine times in less than a year, reducing it from 12.75 percent in April last year to 9.25 percent this month, as price pressures eased significantly from the inflation shock in late 2005.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0745 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       119.37         119.92       +0.46\par  Sing dlr        1.5329         1.5329       -0.00\par  Taiwan dlr      32.978         32.908       -0.21\par  Korean won      936.30         934.60       -0.18\par  Baht             35.70          35.72       +0.06\par  Peso             48.11          48.25       +0.29\par  Rupiah         9048.00        9040.00       -0.09\par  Rupee            44.07          44.11       +0.09\par  Ringgit         3.4920         3.4935       +0.04\par  Yuan            7.7490         7.7545       +0.07\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       119.37         119.01       -0.30\par  Sing dlr        1.5329         1.5337       +0.05\par  Taiwan dlr      32.978         32.585       -1.19\par  Korean won      936.30        0929.50       -0.73\par  Baht             35.70          36.0