\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Jan 18  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,216 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2007\par 09:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0830 GMT. Not all stories appear on all wires. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Bank of Japan's policy board decided Thursday to keep monetary policy steady after facing strong political pressure to do so, but the large number of dissenters at the vote suggested it may move to lift rates next month. ("=2nd UPDATE: BOJ Keeps Policy Steady; Feb Hike Possible," published at 0816 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Bank of Japan's decision to keep monetary policy steady might have pleased the government, but it has exposed a deep split in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board and damaged the BOJ's credibility within financial markets. (BOJ WATCH will be published by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's service sector activity fell in November for the first time in two months, government data showed Thursday, mirroring recently listless consumer spending. ("=UPDATE: Japan Tertiary Data Suggest Sluggish Consumption," published at 0218 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Six-year highs for mortgage rates and rising rents are threatening to leave a generation of aspiring Australian home buyers living with their parents. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: Home Affordability Unlikely To Improve," published at 0459 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves expanded at a modest 1.4% month-on-month pace in December as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation pressures appeared to have reached limits of tolerance, spurring \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly in Thailand and \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserves +1.4% On Ctrl Bk \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," published at 0437 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: With inflationary pressures easing, the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday will likely cut its benchmark overnight interest rates for the first time in over three years to spur economic growth. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Philippines Likely To Cut Rates," published at 0823 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Undermined by the Bank of Japan's policy board decision Thursday to keep monetary policy unchanged, the yen is set to weaken further against the U.S. dollar in coming days. ("=CHARTING ASIA:Dollar May Test Y121.39 Resistance In Days," published at 0526 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong's jobless rate in the three months ended December was unchanged at a six-year low of 4.4%, having declined steadily since April-July 2003 when it peaked at 8.6% amid the Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome outbreak. ("=DATA SNAP: HK Oct-Dec Jobless Rate 4.4%; Mkt Saw 4.4%," published at 0818 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Tanabe Seiyaku Co. said it is in merger talks with Mitsubishi Pharma Corp. in a deal that could create Japan's sixth-largest drugmaker and would highlight the furious pace of mergers and acquisitions in Japan. ("=2nd UPDATE: Tanabe Seiyaku Eyes Mitsubishi Pharma Merger," published at 0120 GMT; 3rd UPDATE will be published by around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Reliance Industries announces fiscal third-quarter results around 1200 GMT. (Story will be published by around 1215 GMT, followed by UPDATE)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Indian pharmaceutical firm Ranbaxy Laboratories Ltd. Thursday said its fourth-quarter net profit more than doubled as strong generic drug sales in the U.S. helped widen its profit margins. ("India Ranbaxy 4Q Net INR1.86 Bln Vs INR696 Mln," published at 0813 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Pursuing its aggressive Asian expansion strategy, Wall Street investment bank Lehman Brothers Holdings Inc. Thursday entered the Australian market with the acquisition of privately-owned Grange Securities. ("=UPDATE: Lehman Bros Buys Australia's Grange Securities," published at 0702 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Blue-chip contract footwear maker Yue Yuen Industrial to announce full-year results after 0830 GMT, with analysts looking for a 13.5% year-on-year rise. (Story will be published after 0830 GMT, likely followed by UPDATE)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Facing criticism about payouts to former executives, AWB Ltd. Thursday said it has renewed its management team and cut salaries in the wake of the Iraqi kickbacks scandal. AWB, which until recently held the export monopoly for Australian wheat, also said it has introduced new corporate governance arrangements to address issues raised by growers. ("=UPDATE: Australia's AWB Under Pressure Over Exec Pay," published at 0313 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Shares of China's Laiwu Steel Corp. rose 54% to CNY9.44 early in their first day of trade in almost four months after being suspended while the company carried out share reform. ("China's Laiwu Steel Soars 54% To CNY9.44 After 4-Mo Halt," published at 0207 GMT; UPDATE will be published by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Creditors of Hynix Semiconductor Inc. may sell up to 18 million shares in the world's third-largest chipmaker by sales, through the market this year to recoup their investments, a Hynix executive said. ("=INTERVIEW:Hynix Creditors May Sell Up To 18M Shrs In 07," published at 0707 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Samsung Electro-Mechanics Co. said Thursday it swung to a fourth-quarter net profit helped by cost-cutting efforts and a rise in sales of electrical components. ("Korea Samsung Electro-Mechanics Turns To Profit In 4Q," published at 0220 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: It may be unrealistic for investors in The Warehouse Group Ltd. to expect potential acquirers of the company to pay much above its current share price for a controlling stake in the company, analysts say. ("=NZ Warehouse Takeover Bid May Not Attract Big Premium," published at 0356 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's antitrust regulator said Thursday it has fined Hyundai Motor Co. KRW23 billion for unfair market practices. ("Hyundai Motor Fined KRW23B For Unfair Practices - FTC," published at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Mitsubishi Fuso Truck and Bus Corp., a truck-making unit of DaimlerChrysler AG , said Thursday it will soon start recalling about 56,000 of its trucks in Japan, due to defective hubs used to attach wheels to axles. ("Mitsubishi Fuso: To Recall 56,000 Trucks In Japan," published at 0238 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Saudi Arabia's oil minister Ali Naimi said Thursday that his country is committed to boosting its supplies of energy to the rest of the world, and aims to have a sustainable oil output capacity of 12.5 million barrels a day by 2009. ("Saudi Output Capacity Target Is 12.5M B/D By 2009 - Naimi," published at 0617 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia's drive to become one of the world's largest biofuel producers is gathering momentum, but it must be carefully monitored to ensure biofuel production doesn't come at the expense of deforestation, and the country mustn't sacrifice its forests to meet a growing global need for clean energy, Greenpeace representatives said Thursday. ("=INTERVIEW:New Biofuel Plans May Hit Indonesian Forests," published at 0729 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Petroleum & Chemical Corp. , Asia's largest refiner by capacity, said Thursday that it refined a record volume of crude oil last year, with output of major chemicals such as ethylene witnessing double-digit growth. ("=UPDATE: Sinopec '06 Oil Refining +4.56%, Chemicals Shine," published at 0325 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australia's Woodside Petroleum Ltd. said Thursday it met its revised full year production forecast with a record output of 67.9 million barrels of oil equivalent in 2006. ("Australia's Woodside FY Output 67.9 Mln Barrels," published at 2255 GMT) [ 18-01-07 0835GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070118e31i000d9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
