\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-S.Korea plans tax breaks on investment to curb won  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lee Jin-joo  \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2007\par 04:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Adds market reaction, economist, details)  \par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Jan 15 (Reuters) - South Korea said on Monday it planned to encourage local companies to invest abroad by giving them tax breaks, the latest in a series of efforts to take pressure off a strong won \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kwon O-kyu told a media briefing that he anticipated a sizeable outflow of funds this year as a result of the move.  \par \par \uc2  "The following measures are expected to see results of an outflow of around $10 billion-$15 billion, which is an outstanding effect to the market," Kwon O-kyu said.  \par \par \uc2  Kwon added it was a conservative estimate, giving room for a possibly larger effect after the proposals are implemented.  \par \par \uc2  The government is to exempt taxes on further marginal profits generated from overseas investment by South Korean investment firms and securities companies, starting from the first quarter of this year for the next three years.  \par \par \uc2  Until recently, the government had deducted tax from income at source at the rate of 14 percent  \par \par \uc2  "The measures are to maintain a balance of the supply and demand in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by promoting an outflow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and adjusting the inflow to an optimum level," the finance ministry said in a statement.  \par \par \uc2  Local financial market showed littled reaction to the news. The won <KRW=> rose slightly to 938.3/8.7 against the dollar from Friday's close of 939.70, treasury bond futures were quoted four ticks lower at 108.15, and the stock market's KOSPI <.KS11> edged up 0.19 percent.  \par \par \uc2  "The announcement didn't have any vast changes in it which could affect the market in the short term. Although, three years of tax-free investment is certainly an attractive proposal and will probably heat up overseas investments eventually," said Ryu Seung-sun, economist at Mirae Asset Securities.  \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, South Korea's Export-Import Bank is to issue $0.1 billion worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds while increasing bonds in won-terms to a total of 1.7 trillion won ($1.81 billion) this year from 0.7 trillion won issued in 2006.  \par \par \uc2  ONE-SIDED POLICY  \par \par \uc2  Financial authorities are looking to follow up on existing measures to stimulate overseas investment and pump more dollars out of the South Korean economy.  \par \par \uc2  "Financial institutions still remain too reluctant in investing abroad. They should be encouraged to invest more abroad," said Rhee Yeung-kyun, a deputy governor of the Bank of Korea, said over lunch with foreign journalists on Monday.  \par \par \uc2  "As you know, we need to pump out (capital)."  \par \par \uc2  However, analysts said that government measures to cool the won could backfire.  \par \par \uc2  "This may be the same as pouring oil into the fire. The government is only thinking about one side of the problem. What if the won suddenly turns lower?" an economist at a local securities firm said.  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast in December that South Korea would post a current surplus of about $2 billion in 2007, marking a record of 10 consecutive years of surplus.  \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves from Asia's third-largest economy jumped $4.7 billion in December for the sixth straight month, data showed, amid dealer reports of heavy government dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at capping the won's rise.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already eased rules on overseas investment in securities and real estate to take some air out of the won, which gained 28 percent in the past three years against the dollar.  ($1=939.7 Won)  (Additional reporting by Yoo Choonsik and Lee Shin-hyung)     \par \par \uc2 KOREA-INVESTMENT/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070115e31f0004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
