\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  won just up despite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2006\par 04:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Dec 28 (Reuters) - The South Korean won <KRW=> inched higher against the dollar on Thursday, scoring gains in the final trading day of an overall bullish year despite what dealers say was heavy government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its strength.  \par \par \uc2  They said the won could have risen much further without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , boosted by exporters' dollar offers and strong current account surplus data released earlier in the day.  \par \par \uc2  The authorities are estimated to have bought nearly $1 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at capping the won's gains.  \par \par \uc2  The won ended domestic session at 929.5/9 per dollar, just above the previous close of 930.3/7. It rose 8.8 percent against the dollar for the year, after gaining 2.3 percent in 2005 and 15.2 percent in 2004, according to closing price provided by the Bank of Korea.  \par \par \uc2  "Despite heavy exporter dollar sales, the government was committed to defend the dollar around 930," a dealer at a foreign bank said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed the seasonally adjusted current account surplus quadrupled to its biggest in nearly two years in November, helped by robust exports and giving an additional boost to the already strong won. [ID:nSEO152981]  \par \par \uc2  The yen <JPY=> crept higher against the dollar on Thursday on caution that the Bank of Japan could raise interest rates as early as January.  \par \par \uc2  The yen was last at 118.76/78 per dollar, compared with 118.85 yen in late U.S. trading. It scaled a two-month low of 119.23 per dollar on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2  The Seoul stock market's benchmark KOSPI <.KS11> ended up 0.66 percent. Foreign investors ended the year's last session as net sellers, dumping a net 21 billion won worth of shares on the main board.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                         0600 GMT     0310 GMT   prev close\par  Won <KRW=>                 929.5/9      929.6/9      930.3/7\par  Yen/won <JPYKRW=R>      7.8241/294   7.8188/246   7.8269/322\par  KOSPI <.KS11>             1,434.46     1,428.20     1,425.10\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-KOREA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061231e2cs0001n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
